// Original question set (migrated with stable ids). New questions live in the per-topic files.
SF.add("apex", [["apex-001","Basic","concept","What are governor limits and why do they exist?","<p>Salesforce is multi-tenant, so each transaction gets hard caps to stop one org's code from starving others. Key synchronous limits:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Limit</th><th>Sync</th><th>Async</th></tr>\n<tr><td>SOQL queries</td><td>100</td><td>200</td></tr><tr><td>Records retrieved by SOQL</td><td>50,000</td><td>50,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DML statements</td><td>150</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td>Records processed by DML</td><td>10,000</td><td>10,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CPU time</td><td>10 s</td><td>60 s</td></tr><tr><td>Heap size</td><td>6 MB</td><td>12 MB</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Callouts</td><td>100</td><td>100</td></tr></table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\">Use <code>Limits.getQueries()</code> / <code>Limits.getLimitQueries()</code> to inspect consumption at runtime.</p>"],
["apex-002","Basic","concept","Difference between List, Set and Map in Apex?","<ul><li><b>List</b>: ordered, allows duplicates, index access. Default return of SOQL.</li>\n<li><b>Set</b>: unordered, unique values. Ideal for collecting Ids to use in a <code>WHERE Id IN :ids</code> filter.</li>\n<li><b>Map</b>: key → value. <code>new Map&lt;Id, Account&gt;([SELECT ...])</code> builds an Id-keyed map in one line; essential for bulk lookups in triggers.</li></ul>"],
["apex-003","Basic","concept","What is the difference between SOQL and SOSL?","<p><b>SOQL</b> queries one object (plus relationships) with precise filters; returns a List of records. <b>SOSL</b> does a text search across multiple objects and fields using the search index; returns <code>List&lt;List&lt;SObject&gt;&gt;</code>.</p>\n<p>Use SOSL when you don't know which object/field contains the term (global search, email/phone lookup across Leads and Contacts). SOSL limit is 20 queries per transaction and 2,000 rows.</p>"],
["apex-004","Mid","concept","Explain Database.insert(records, false) vs insert records.","<p><code>insert</code> is all-or-nothing: one bad record throws <code>DmlException</code> and rolls back the whole statement. <code>Database.insert(list, false)</code> allows partial success and returns <code>Database.SaveResult[]</code> that you loop to log errors.</p>\n<pre><code>Database.SaveResult[] res = Database.insert(accs, false);\nfor (Integer i = 0; i &lt; res.size(); i++) {\n  if (!res[i].isSuccess()) {\n    for (Database.Error e : res[i].getErrors())\n      System.debug(accs[i].Name + ': ' + e.getMessage());\n  }\n}</code></pre>"],
["apex-005","Mid","concept","What is a Mixed DML error and how do you resolve it?","<p>You get <code>MIXED_DML_OPERATION</code> when a single transaction does DML on a <b>setup object</b> (User, Group, GroupMember, PermissionSetAssignment, UserRole) and a <b>non-setup object</b> (Account, Contact…), because setup changes affect sharing recalculation.</p>\n<ul><li>Move one side into a <code>@future</code> method or Queueable.</li>\n<li>In tests, wrap the setup DML in <code>System.runAs(user) { ... }</code>.</li></ul>"],
["apex-006","Mid","concept","Explain static vs instance variables and how static behaves in a transaction.","<p>A static variable belongs to the class and lives for the duration of a single <b>transaction</b> (not across requests, unlike Java). That makes it the standard tool for trigger recursion guards and per-transaction caches. Instance variables live as long as the object instance.</p>"],
["apex-007","Mid","concept","What are the different exception handling best practices in Apex?","<ul><li>Catch specific exceptions (<code>DmlException</code>, <code>QueryException</code>, <code>CalloutException</code>) before generic <code>Exception</code>.</li>\n<li>Create custom exceptions: <code>public class OrderException extends Exception {}</code>.</li>\n<li>Log to a custom object or Platform Event (events publish even if the transaction rolls back, when using \"Publish Immediately\").</li>\n<li>Use <code>addError()</code> in triggers to show user-friendly messages instead of throwing.</li>\n<li>Never swallow exceptions silently in a catch block.</li></ul>"],
["apex-008","Mid","concept","What is the difference between WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED, WITH USER_MODE and Security.stripInaccessible?","<ul><li><code>WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED</code>: throws if any field/object in the SELECT is inaccessible. Does not cover WHERE clause fields. Older approach.</li>\n<li><code>WITH USER_MODE</code> (and <code>insert as user</code>): runs the query/DML with the running user's CRUD, FLS and sharing. Covers WHERE too, reports all errors. Current recommended approach.</li>\n<li><code>Security.stripInaccessible(AccessType.READABLE, records)</code>: silently removes fields the user can't access, so the code continues gracefully.</li></ul>"],
["apex-009","Mid","concept","How do you write a dynamic SOQL query safely?","<p>Use <code>Database.query()</code> or better <code>Database.queryWithBinds()</code> with a bind map, and escape user input to prevent SOQL injection.</p>\n<pre><code>Map&lt;String,Object&gt; binds = new Map&lt;String,Object&gt;{'nm' =&gt; '%' + searchKey + '%'};\nList&lt;Account&gt; r = Database.queryWithBinds(\n  'SELECT Id, Name FROM Account WHERE Name LIKE :nm',\n  binds, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);</code></pre>\n<p>For field/object names coming from input, whitelist them against <code>Schema.getGlobalDescribe()</code>; <code>String.escapeSingleQuotes()</code> only protects string literals.</p>"],
["apex-010","Mid","concept","What are interfaces and abstract classes used for in real Salesforce projects?","<p>Interfaces define a contract: <code>Database.Batchable</code>, <code>Queueable</code>, <code>Schedulable</code>, <code>HttpCalloutMock</code>, and custom ones like a <code>ITriggerHandler</code>. Abstract classes provide shared base behaviour, for example a <code>TriggerHandler</code> base class with virtual <code>beforeInsert()</code> etc. Combined with Custom Metadata, interfaces enable strategy patterns: <code>Type.forName(className).newInstance()</code> picks an implementation at runtime without code changes.</p>"],
["apex-011","Basic","concept","What are the key rules for Apex test classes?","<ul><li>75% org-wide coverage to deploy to production; every trigger needs some coverage.</li>\n<li>Tests don't see org data by default (<code>SeeAllData=false</code>); create data in <code>@TestSetup</code>.</li>\n<li>Use <code>Test.startTest()/stopTest()</code> to get fresh limits and force async code to run.</li>\n<li>Assert outcomes with <code>Assert.areEqual</code>, test bulk (200 records), positive, negative and user-permission cases with <code>System.runAs</code>.</li>\n<li>Mock callouts with <code>Test.setMock</code>.</li></ul>"],
["apex-012","Mid","concept","How do you test a callout?","<pre><code>@isTest\nglobal class AcctMock implements HttpCalloutMock {\n  global HTTPResponse respond(HTTPRequest req) {\n    HttpResponse res = new HttpResponse();\n    res.setStatusCode(200);\n    res.setBody('{\"status\":\"ok\"}');\n    return res;\n  }\n}\n// in test\nTest.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, new AcctMock());\nTest.startTest();\nMyService.sync(accId);\nTest.stopTest();</code></pre>\n<p>For multiple endpoints use a mock that branches on <code>req.getEndpoint()</code>, or <code>MultiStaticResourceCalloutMock</code>. SOAP uses <code>WebServiceMock</code>.</p>"],
["apex-013","Senior","concept","How do you avoid ‘Too many SOQL queries: 101’?","<ul><li>Never query or do DML inside loops. Collect Ids into a Set, query once, store in a Map.</li>\n<li>Use relationship (parent-child) queries to fetch related data in a single query.</li>\n<li>Cache results in static variables within the transaction.</li>\n<li>Check for trigger recursion and other automation (flows, process builders) firing the same code multiple times.</li>\n<li>Move heavy work to async contexts where the limit is 200.</li></ul>"],
["apex-014","Senior","concept","What is the Apex Enterprise Patterns (fflib) layering?","<p>Separation of concerns into layers:</p><ul><li><b>Service</b>: business operations, transaction boundary, called by UI, API, batch.</li>\n<li><b>Domain</b>: object-specific logic and validation (trigger logic lives here).</li>\n<li><b>Selector</b>: all SOQL for an object, consistent field lists, security enforcement.</li>\n<li><b>Unit of Work</b>: collects DML and commits in correct order in one go.</li></ul>\n<p>Benefits: testability (mocking with ApexMocks), reuse, fewer duplicated queries.</p>"],
["apex-015","Mid","concept","What are Custom Metadata Types vs Custom Settings?","<ul><li><b>Custom Metadata</b>: records are metadata, so they deploy with change sets/packages; queries don't count towards SOQL limits (via <code>getAll()</code>/<code>getInstance()</code>); support relationships and validation. Best for config like mapping tables, endpoints, feature toggles.</li>\n<li><b>Custom Settings</b>: data, not deployable as records. Hierarchy settings can vary by org/profile/user; list settings are largely legacy.</li></ul>"],
["apex-016","Senior","concept","How do you handle large data volumes in Apex (beyond 50,000 rows)?","<ul><li>Use Batch Apex with <code>Database.QueryLocator</code> (up to 50 million rows).</li>\n<li>Use SOQL for loops (<code>for (List&lt;Account&gt; chunk : [SELECT ...])</code>) to process in chunks of 200 and save heap.</li>\n<li>Write selective queries on indexed fields (Id, Name, External Id, lookups, CreatedDate) to avoid non-selective query errors on objects over 200k rows.</li>\n<li>Use aggregate queries instead of pulling all rows; consider skinny tables and custom indexes via Support.</li></ul>"],
["apex-017","Mid","concept","What is the difference between with sharing, without sharing and inherited sharing?","<ul><li><b>with sharing</b>: enforces the running user's record-level sharing.</li>\n<li><b>without sharing</b>: ignores sharing (system context). Use deliberately, e.g. a utility that must read all records.</li>\n<li><b>inherited sharing</b>: takes the sharing mode of the caller; if it's the entry point it runs with sharing. Recommended default for service classes.</li></ul>\n<p>Note: none of these enforce CRUD/FLS; that needs <code>USER_MODE</code> or <code>stripInaccessible</code>.</p>"],
["apex-018","Senior","concept","How do you make Apex code testable with dependency injection and stubs?","<pre><code>public with sharing class InvoiceService {\n  @TestVisible private static IPaymentGateway gateway = new StripeGateway();\n  public static void charge(Invoice__c inv) { gateway.charge(inv.Amount__c, inv.Token__c); }\n}\n// Test using the Stub API\n@isTest static void chargesGateway() {\n  GatewayStub stub = new GatewayStub();\n  InvoiceService.gateway = (IPaymentGateway) Test.createStub(IPaymentGateway.class, stub);\n  InvoiceService.charge(new Invoice__c(Amount__c = 10));\n  Assert.areEqual(1, stub.calls);\n}\npublic class GatewayStub implements System.StubProvider {\n  public Integer calls = 0;\n  public Object handleMethodCall(Object o, String m, Type r, List&lt;Type&gt; pt,\n                                 List&lt;String&gt; pn, List&lt;Object&gt; args) { calls++; return null; }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Benefits: fast unit tests without DML/callouts, isolated failures. Selectors can be stubbed the same way to avoid creating data.</p>"],
["apex-019","Senior","design","How do you design an org-wide logging framework?","<ul><li>Logger class buffers entries in a static list during the transaction.</li>\n<li>Flush publishes a <code>Log_Event__e</code> platform event with \"Publish Immediately\", so logs survive a rollback.</li>\n<li>An event trigger writes <code>Log__c</code> records (async, as Automated Process user).</li>\n<li>Capture class, method, record Id, user, transaction Id (<code>Request.getCurrent().getRequestId()</code>), quiddity (<code>Request.getCurrent().getQuiddity()</code> tells you if it's a batch, trigger, REST call...).</li>\n<li>Log level controlled by Custom Metadata per user/class; purge job deletes old logs.</li>\n<li>Open source: Nebula Logger is a mature reference.</li></ul>"],
["apex-020","Senior","concept","How would you reduce CPU time in a trigger handling 200 records with complex logic?","<ul><li>Replace nested loops with Maps keyed by Id or composite keys.</li>\n<li>Cache describe calls (<code>Schema.getGlobalDescribe()</code> is expensive); use <code>Account.SObjectType.getDescribe()</code> or <code>Schema.describeSObjects</code> once.</li>\n<li>Avoid JSON serialize/deserialize in loops and regex on large strings.</li>\n<li>Filter records early: only process those whose relevant fields changed.</li>\n<li>Remember CPU includes time spent in flows and managed package code in the same transaction; DB time (SOQL execution) isn't counted.</li>\n<li>Measure with <code>Limits.getCpuTime()</code> before/after sections.</li></ul>"]]);
SF.add("trigger", [["trigger-001","Basic","concept","What trigger events and context variables are available?","<p>Events: before/after insert, before/after update, before/after delete, after undelete.</p>\n<p>Context variables: <code>Trigger.new</code>, <code>Trigger.old</code>, <code>Trigger.newMap</code>, <code>Trigger.oldMap</code>, <code>Trigger.isInsert/isUpdate/isDelete/isUndelete</code>, <code>isBefore/isAfter</code>, <code>Trigger.size</code>, <code>Trigger.operationType</code> (enum, useful in switch statements).</p>\n<p><code>Trigger.new</code> isn't available in delete; <code>Trigger.old</code> isn't available in insert; <code>newMap</code> isn't available in before insert because records have no Id yet.</p>"],
["trigger-002","Basic","scenario","When do you use a before trigger vs an after trigger?","<ul><li><b>Before</b>: modify fields on the same records (no DML needed), validation with <code>addError()</code>.</li>\n<li><b>After</b>: need the record Id or system fields (CreatedDate), create/update related records, callouts via async, rollups to parents.</li></ul>\n<p>Updating <code>Trigger.new</code> in an after trigger throws a read-only error.</p>"],
["trigger-003","Mid","concept","Explain the Salesforce order of execution.","<ol style=\"padding-left:20px;margin:0 0 10px\"><li>Load original record / initialize new values</li><li>System validation (layout required fields, field formats)</li><li>Before-save record-triggered flows</li><li>Before triggers</li><li>System validation again + custom validation rules</li><li>Duplicate rules</li><li>Record saved (not committed)</li><li>After triggers</li><li>Assignment rules, auto-response rules</li><li>Workflow rules (if field update: before/after update triggers fire once more)</li><li>Escalation rules</li><li>After-save record-triggered flows (and legacy Process Builder)</li><li>Entitlement rules</li><li>Roll-up summary / cross-object workflow on parent (parent goes through its own save)</li><li>Criteria-based sharing evaluation</li><li>Commit to database</li><li>Post-commit: emails, async Apex enqueued, outbound messages</li></ol>"],
["trigger-004","Mid","concept","How do you prevent trigger recursion?","<p>A simple static Boolean blocks the whole re-entry, which breaks bulk operations over 200 records (chunks). A better approach is a static Set of processed Ids:</p>\n<pre><code>public class AccountTriggerHelper {\n  public static Set&lt;Id&gt; processed = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\n}\n// in handler (after update)\nfor (Account a : newList) {\n  if (AccountTriggerHelper.processed.contains(a.Id)) continue;\n  AccountTriggerHelper.processed.add(a.Id);\n  // logic\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Even better: only act when the relevant field actually changed (<code>a.Status__c != oldMap.get(a.Id).Status__c</code>).</p>"],
["trigger-005","Mid","concept","Why use a trigger handler framework? Describe one.","<p>One trigger per object, no logic in the trigger, routing to a handler class. Benefits: controlled order, testability, bypass capability, consistency.</p>\n<pre><code>trigger AccountTrigger on Account (before insert, before update, after insert, after update) {\n  new AccountTriggerHandler().run();\n}\npublic virtual class TriggerHandler {\n  public void run() {\n    if (isBypassed()) return;\n    switch on Trigger.operationType {\n      when BEFORE_INSERT { beforeInsert(); }\n      when AFTER_UPDATE  { afterUpdate(); }\n      // ...\n    }\n  }\n  protected virtual void beforeInsert() {}\n  protected virtual void afterUpdate() {}\n  private Boolean isBypassed() { /* check Custom Metadata / Custom Permission */ return false; }\n}</code></pre>"],
["trigger-006","Mid","concept","How do you bypass triggers for a data load?","<ul><li>Custom Metadata or Hierarchy Custom Setting flag checked in the handler (per user/profile).</li>\n<li>Custom Permission assigned via a permission set: <code>FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Bypass_Triggers')</code>.</li>\n<li>Static variable bypass inside code (<code>TriggerHandler.bypass('AccountTriggerHandler')</code>) for programmatic flows.</li></ul>"],
["trigger-007","Mid","code","Write a trigger to update the count of Contacts on Account.","<pre><code>trigger ContactTrigger on Contact (after insert, after update, after delete, after undelete) {\n  Set&lt;Id&gt; accIds = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\n  if (Trigger.new != null) for (Contact c : Trigger.new) if (c.AccountId != null) accIds.add(c.AccountId);\n  if (Trigger.old != null) for (Contact c : Trigger.old) if (c.AccountId != null) accIds.add(c.AccountId);\n  if (accIds.isEmpty()) return;\n\n  Map&lt;Id,Account&gt; toUpdate = new Map&lt;Id,Account&gt;();\n  for (Id i : accIds) toUpdate.put(i, new Account(Id = i, Contact_Count__c = 0));\n  for (AggregateResult ar : [SELECT AccountId aid, COUNT(Id) c FROM Contact\n                             WHERE AccountId IN :accIds GROUP BY AccountId]) {\n    toUpdate.get((Id) ar.get('aid')).Contact_Count__c = (Integer) ar.get('c');\n  }\n  update toUpdate.values();\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Handles reparenting (old and new Account) and delete/undelete. In a real org, move this into a handler.</p>"],
["trigger-008","Mid","concept","How do you show an error on a specific field from a trigger?","<p><code>record.FieldName__c.addError('Message')</code> highlights the field; <code>record.addError('Message')</code> shows a page-level error. Both block the save for that record only, so partial success with <code>Database.insert(list,false)</code> still works for the others.</p>"],
["trigger-009","Senior","concept","Trigger vs Record-Triggered Flow — how do you decide?","<ul><li><b>Before-save flow</b>: fast same-record field updates, admin-maintainable. Often replaces simple before triggers.</li>\n<li><b>After-save flow</b>: simple related record creation, emails, standard actions.</li>\n<li><b>Apex trigger</b>: complex logic, heavy data volume, maps/aggregations, callouts orchestration, strict ordering, reusable unit-tested logic.</li></ul>\n<p>The real issue is mixing both on one object without a design. Define an automation strategy per object and use Flow Trigger Explorer to set order.</p>"],
["trigger-010","Mid","concept","Can you make a callout from a trigger?","<p>Not synchronously — triggers run in an uncommitted transaction. Use <code>@future(callout=true)</code>, a Queueable implementing <code>Database.AllowsCallouts</code>, or publish a Platform Event whose subscriber does the callout. Queueable is preferred: it accepts sObjects and supports chaining and monitoring.</p>"],
["trigger-011","Basic","concept","What happens to triggers during a Data Loader load of 10,000 records?","<p>Records are processed in chunks of 200 (or the batch size set in Data Loader, max 10,000 in Bulk API). The trigger fires once per chunk, and each chunk is a separate transaction with its own limits in Bulk API. That's why logic must be bulkified and static recursion flags must be Id-based.</p>"],
["trigger-012","Senior","concept","Which operations do NOT fire triggers?","<ul><li>Cascade delete of child records in master-detail (children's delete triggers don't fire; parent's does).</li>\n<li>Mass campaign status changes, mass division transfer, address picklist conversions.</li>\n<li>Renaming picklist values, changing a record's owner via some mass transfer tools.</li>\n<li>Updates to fields by the system for formula recalculation.</li></ul>\n<p>Also: Opportunity line item changes fire triggers on OLI, and rollups cause the parent to be saved (firing its triggers).</p>"],
["trigger-013","Mid","concept","How do you identify which fields changed in an update trigger?","<pre><code>for (Opportunity o : (List&lt;Opportunity&gt;) Trigger.new) {\n  Opportunity old = (Opportunity) Trigger.oldMap.get(o.Id);\n  if (o.StageName != old.StageName &amp;&amp; o.StageName == 'Closed Won') {\n    // act only on transition\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Generic version: loop <code>Schema.SObjectType.Opportunity.fields.getMap().keySet()</code> and compare <code>o.get(f) != old.get(f)</code> (expensive, use sparingly).</p>"],
["trigger-014","Senior","design","Two triggers and a flow on Opportunity all update the Account. Users get intermittent errors. How do you redesign?","<ul><li>Consolidate to one trigger per object with a handler; move the flow logic into the handler or make the flow a before-save flow on Opportunity only.</li>\n<li>Collect Account updates into a single Map and do one DML at the end (Unit of Work).</li>\n<li>Only update Account when values actually change, to prevent the Account triggers and its own automations from cascading.</li>\n<li>Look at the errors: <code>UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW</code> suggests contention; recursion errors suggest the cascade; CPU errors suggest duplicate processing.</li>\n<li>Document an automation map per object and set Flow trigger order.</li></ul>"],
["trigger-015","Senior","concept","How do you write a metadata-driven trigger framework where admins can turn handlers on/off and set order?","<pre><code>// Custom Metadata: Trigger_Handler__mdt\n//   Object__c, Handler_Class__c, Order__c, Active__c, Bypass_Permission__c\npublic class MetadataTriggerDispatcher {\n  public static void run(SObjectType t) {\n    for (Trigger_Handler__mdt h : [SELECT Handler_Class__c, Bypass_Permission__c\n         FROM Trigger_Handler__mdt WHERE Object__c = :String.valueOf(t) AND Active__c = true\n         ORDER BY Order__c]) {\n      if (h.Bypass_Permission__c != null &amp;&amp; FeatureManagement.checkPermission(h.Bypass_Permission__c)) continue;\n      ITriggerHandler handler = (ITriggerHandler) Type.forName(h.Handler_Class__c).newInstance();\n      handler.handle(Trigger.operationType, Trigger.new, Trigger.oldMap);\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Custom Metadata queries don't count against SOQL limits. Lets you deploy a handler inactive and switch it on without code changes.</p>"]]);
SF.add("async", [["async-001","Basic","concept","Compare Future, Queueable, Batch and Schedulable.","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th></th><th>Future</th><th>Queueable</th><th>Batch</th><th>Schedulable</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Params</td><td>Primitives only</td><td>Any type incl. sObjects</td><td>Query/Iterable</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Job Id / monitoring</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes (CronTrigger)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chaining</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (1 child in async)</td><td>Call next batch in finish()</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Volume</td><td>Small</td><td>Medium</td><td>Up to 50M records</td><td>Starts other jobs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Callouts</td><td>callout=true</td><td>AllowsCallouts</td><td>AllowsCallouts</td><td>Via other async</td></tr></table></div>"],
["async-002","Basic","concept","Why can't @future methods accept sObjects?","<p>The sObject could change between the time the call is made and when the method executes, so you'd work with stale data. Pass Ids and re-query, or pass serialized JSON. Queueable removes this restriction.</p>"],
["async-003","Mid","concept","Can you call a future method from another future method or a batch?","<p>No. A future method cannot call another future method, and you can't call a future from Batch Apex (throws an exception). Use Queueable instead. Also check <code>System.isFuture()</code> / <code>System.isBatch()</code> before enqueuing from shared code.</p>"],
["async-004","Mid","concept","Explain the Batch Apex lifecycle.","<pre><code>public class AccountCleanup implements Database.Batchable&lt;SObject&gt;, Database.Stateful {\n  Integer processed = 0;\n  public Database.QueryLocator start(Database.BatchableContext bc) {\n    return Database.getQueryLocator('SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Inactive__c = true');\n  }\n  public void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List&lt;Account&gt; scope) {\n    // each chunk = its own transaction with fresh limits\n    processed += scope.size();\n  }\n  public void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) {\n    // send summary email, chain next job\n  }\n}\nDatabase.executeBatch(new AccountCleanup(), 200);</code></pre>\n<p>Default scope 200, max 2,000. Up to 5 batch jobs active at once (more wait in the Apex flex queue, up to 100).</p>"],
["async-005","Mid","concept","What does Database.Stateful do?","<p>By default instance variables reset between <code>execute()</code> chunks. Implementing <code>Database.Stateful</code> preserves them, so you can accumulate counters or error lists to use in <code>finish()</code>. It adds serialization cost, so keep stateful data small.</p>"],
["async-006","Mid","concept","QueryLocator vs Iterable in Batch start()?","<ul><li><b>QueryLocator</b>: simple SOQL, up to 50 million records, bypasses the 50k row limit.</li>\n<li><b>Iterable</b>: custom logic, data from callouts or complex filtering, but subject to normal governor limits (50k rows).</li></ul>"],
["async-007","Mid","concept","How do you chain Queueable jobs and what are the limits?","<pre><code>public class StepOne implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {\n  public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {\n    // work...\n    if (!Test.isRunningTest()) System.enqueueJob(new StepTwo());\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>From a synchronous transaction you can enqueue 50 jobs; from inside a Queueable only 1 child. Chain depth is unlimited in production (Developer Edition limits it to 5). You can also set <code>AsyncOptions</code> with a <code>MaximumQueueableStackDepth</code> and a delay of 0–10 minutes.</p>"],
["async-008","Mid","concept","What is the Transaction Finalizer in Queueable?","<p>A class implementing <code>System.Finalizer</code> attached via <code>System.attachFinalizer()</code>. It runs after the Queueable finishes, even if it failed with an uncatchable error (like a limit exception). Used for retry logic and error logging.</p>\n<pre><code>public class RetryFinalizer implements Finalizer {\n  public void execute(FinalizerContext ctx) {\n    if (ctx.getResult() == ParentJobResult.UNHANDLED_EXCEPTION) {\n      // log ctx.getException(), re-enqueue with retry count\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>"],
["async-009","Basic","concept","How do you schedule Apex?","<pre><code>public class NightlySync implements Schedulable {\n  public void execute(SchedulableContext sc) {\n    Database.executeBatch(new AccountCleanup(), 200);\n  }\n}\n// Seconds Minutes Hours Day_of_month Month Day_of_week Year\nSystem.schedule('Nightly sync', '0 0 2 * * ?', new NightlySync());</code></pre>\n<p>Max 100 scheduled jobs. A scheduled class can't make callouts directly; it starts a batch or queueable that does. Classes that are scheduled can't be edited until the job is aborted.</p>"],
["async-010","Mid","concept","How do you test asynchronous Apex?","<p>Wrap the call in <code>Test.startTest()</code> / <code>Test.stopTest()</code>; all async work enqueued in between runs synchronously at <code>stopTest()</code>. For batch, only one execute() runs in tests, so keep test data ≤ scope size. For chained queueables, guard with <code>Test.isRunningTest()</code> or test each class separately.</p>"],
["async-011","Mid","concept","What are Platform Events and when do you use them?","<p>Event-driven messaging on the Event Bus. Publish with <code>EventBus.publish()</code>, Flow, or API; subscribe with Apex triggers (after insert), Flow, LWC (empApi), or external CometD/Pub/Sub API clients.</p>\n<ul><li>Decouple systems (publish an Order_Created__e event, ERP subscribes).</li>\n<li>Error logging that survives rollback (\"Publish Immediately\" behaviour).</li>\n<li>Avoid mixed DML or move work to a separate transaction.</li></ul>\n<p>Platform event triggers run as the Automated Process user, in batches up to 2,000; use <code>setResumeCheckpoint()</code> for resilience.</p>"],
["async-012","Senior","concept","How would you process 5 million records nightly with external callouts?","<ul><li>Batch Apex with QueryLocator, scope ~ 50–100 so each execute stays within 100 callouts and 120 s total callout time.</li>\n<li>Implement <code>Database.AllowsCallouts</code> and <code>Database.Stateful</code> for error tracking.</li>\n<li>Better: bulk the payload — one callout per chunk with many records, if the API supports it.</li>\n<li>Track sync status with a field so failures can be retried by the next run.</li>\n<li>If the target is high-volume, consider middleware (MuleSoft) pulling via Bulk API 2.0 instead of Salesforce pushing.</li></ul>"],
["async-013","Senior","concept","What is the Apex Flex Queue?","<p>A holding area for up to 100 batch jobs in \"Holding\" status when the 5 concurrent active batch slots are full. You can reorder jobs in Setup or with <code>FlexQueue.moveJobToFront()</code>. Monitor via <code>AsyncApexJob</code>.</p>"],
["async-014","Senior","concept","Queueable vs Batch vs Platform Event for processing an incoming file of 100,000 rows — compare.","<ul><li><b>Batch (Iterable over parsed rows or staged records)</b>: predictable chunking, 200-row transactions, built-in monitoring. Best when rows are first staged into a custom object (e.g. via Bulk API).</li>\n<li><b>Chained Queueable</b>: flexible, holds state in member variables, good for a few thousand rows; long chains are harder to monitor.</li>\n<li><b>Platform Events</b>: publish one event per row/chunk; subscriber trigger processes up to 2,000 per batch with automatic retry (<code>EventBus.RetryableException</code>); scales but harder to reason about order and errors.</li></ul>\n<p>Typical answer: stage with Bulk API 2.0 into a staging object, then Batch Apex to validate and upsert into target objects, logging failures.</p>"],
["async-015","Senior","concept","How do you prevent two instances of the same batch job running at once?","<pre><code>Integer running = [SELECT COUNT() FROM AsyncApexJob\n  WHERE ApexClass.Name = 'AccountSyncBatch'\n  AND Status IN ('Holding','Queued','Preparing','Processing')];\nif (running == 0) Database.executeBatch(new AccountSyncBatch(), 200);</code></pre>\n<p>There's still a small race window; for stricter control use a lock record with <code>FOR UPDATE</code> in a custom object, set at start and cleared in finish().</p>"]]);
SF.add("lwc", [["lwc-001","Basic","concept","What files make up an LWC and what is the meta.xml for?","<p><code>myCmp.html</code> (template), <code>myCmp.js</code> (class extending LightningElement), <code>myCmp.js-meta.xml</code> (config), optionally CSS and SVG icon. The meta file sets <code>isExposed</code>, <code>targets</code> (lightning__RecordPage, lightning__AppPage, lightningCommunity__Page, lightning__FlowScreen, etc.), <code>targetConfigs</code> for design properties, and API version.</p>"],
["lwc-002","Basic","concept","Explain decorators @api, @track and @wire.","<ul><li><b>@api</b>: public property/method, settable by parent or App Builder. Reactive.</li>\n<li><b>@track</b>: deep reactivity for object/array mutations. Since Spring '20 all fields are reactive on reassignment, so <code>@track</code> is only needed when mutating nested properties in place.</li>\n<li><b>@wire</b>: reactive data provisioning from an LDS adapter or Apex method marked <code>@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)</code>.</li></ul>"],
["lwc-003","Mid","concept","Wire vs imperative Apex calls — when to use each?","<ul><li><b>@wire</b>: read-only data, automatic re-fetch when reactive params (<code>$recordId</code>) change, cached client-side. Requires <code>cacheable=true</code>, so no DML.</li>\n<li><b>Imperative</b>: DML, user-triggered actions (button click), controlled timing, or when you need fresh non-cached data.</li></ul>\n<pre><code>import getContacts from '@salesforce/apex/ContactCtrl.getContacts';\n@wire(getContacts, { accountId: '$recordId' }) contacts;\n\nasync handleSave() {\n  try { await saveContact({ c: this.record }); }\n  catch (e) { this.error = e.body?.message; }\n}</code></pre>"],
["lwc-004","Mid","concept","How do you refresh wired data after a DML?","<p>Store the wired result and call <code>refreshApex()</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>wiredResult;\n@wire(getContacts, { accountId: '$recordId' })\nwired(result) { this.wiredResult = result; if (result.data) this.rows = result.data; }\n\nasync afterSave() { await refreshApex(this.wiredResult); }</code></pre>\n<p>For LDS-based data (getRecord) after an Apex update, call <code>notifyRecordUpdateAvailable([{recordId}])</code>.</p>"],
["lwc-005","Mid","concept","How do components communicate in LWC?","<ul><li><b>Parent → child</b>: @api properties or @api methods (via <code>this.template.querySelector('c-child').method()</code>).</li>\n<li><b>Child → parent</b>: <code>this.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('select', { detail: id }))</code>; parent listens with <code>onselect</code>.</li>\n<li><b>Unrelated components</b>: Lightning Message Service (works across LWC, Aura, Visualforce).</li>\n<li>Pub-sub module is legacy; use LMS.</li></ul>"],
["lwc-006","Mid","concept","Explain event propagation: bubbles and composed.","<ul><li><code>bubbles:false, composed:false</code> (default): only the immediate parent's handler on the component tag receives it. Recommended.</li>\n<li><code>bubbles:true, composed:false</code>: bubbles through the owner's shadow tree, not past the shadow boundary.</li>\n<li><code>bubbles:true, composed:true</code>: crosses shadow boundaries all the way up. Avoid; it leaks implementation details and can collide with other events.</li></ul>"],
["lwc-007","Basic","concept","What are LWC lifecycle hooks?","<ul><li><code>constructor()</code>: call <code>super()</code> first; don't access attributes or child elements.</li>\n<li><code>connectedCallback()</code>: inserted into DOM; init data, subscribe to LMS.</li>\n<li><code>renderedCallback()</code>: after every render; guard with a flag to avoid loops. Use for third-party libs.</li>\n<li><code>disconnectedCallback()</code>: cleanup, unsubscribe.</li>\n<li><code>errorCallback(error, stack)</code>: error boundary for child components.</li></ul>"],
["lwc-008","Mid","concept","What is Lightning Data Service and its benefits?","<p>LDS provides record CRUD without Apex: <code>lightning-record-form</code>, <code>lightning-record-edit-form</code>, <code>getRecord</code>, <code>createRecord</code>, <code>updateRecord</code>, <code>deleteRecord</code>, <code>getRelatedListRecords</code>. Benefits: shared client cache across components (one update refreshes all), automatic CRUD/FLS and sharing enforcement, no test classes needed.</p>"],
["lwc-009","Mid","concept","How do you use Lightning Message Service?","<pre><code>// messageChannels/OrderSelected.messageChannel-meta.xml defines the channel\nimport { publish, subscribe, MessageContext, APPLICATION_SCOPE } from 'lightning/messageService';\nimport ORDER from '@salesforce/messageChannel/OrderSelected__c';\n\n@wire(MessageContext) ctx;\npublish(this.ctx, ORDER, { orderId: this.id });\n\nconnectedCallback() {\n  this.sub = subscribe(this.ctx, ORDER, (m) =&gt; this.load(m.orderId), { scope: APPLICATION_SCOPE });\n}\ndisconnectedCallback() { unsubscribe(this.sub); }</code></pre>"],
["lwc-010","Mid","concept","How do you navigate and show toasts in LWC?","<pre><code>import { NavigationMixin } from 'lightning/navigation';\nimport { ShowToastEvent } from 'lightning/platformShowToastEvent';\nexport default class Nav extends NavigationMixin(LightningElement) {\n  open(id) {\n    this[NavigationMixin.Navigate]({\n      type: 'standard__recordPage',\n      attributes: { recordId: id, objectApiName: 'Account', actionName: 'view' }\n    });\n    this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({ title: 'Saved', message: 'Account updated', variant: 'success' }));\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Note: ShowToastEvent doesn't display in Experience Cloud LWR sites or Visualforce; use <code>lightning/toast</code> or a custom toast there.</p>"],
["lwc-011","Mid","concept","Why doesn't my UI update when I push to an array?","<p>Mutating an array in place (<code>this.items.push(x)</code>) on a non-<code>@track</code> field doesn't trigger a re-render. Reassign instead: <code>this.items = [...this.items, x]</code>. Same for objects: <code>this.obj = { ...this.obj, name: 'X' }</code>.</p>"],
["lwc-012","Senior","concept","How do you optimise LWC performance?","<ul><li>Use LDS/cacheable Apex and avoid duplicate server calls.</li>\n<li>Lazy-load heavy sections with <code>lwc:if</code> and dynamic imports (<code>lwc:component</code> with <code>lwc:is</code>).</li>\n<li>Paginate or use infinite loading in <code>lightning-datatable</code> instead of loading thousands of rows.</li>\n<li>Debounce search inputs (setTimeout ~300 ms).</li>\n<li>Use <code>key</code> in iterations, avoid heavy getters that recompute on every render.</li>\n<li>Return only needed fields from Apex (wrapper/DTO).</li></ul>"],
["lwc-013","Mid","concept","What is Lightning Locker vs Lightning Web Security?","<p>Both isolate component namespaces for security. Locker wraps DOM/global objects in secure proxies (restricting many third-party libs). <b>Lightning Web Security (LWS)</b> is the modern replacement using JavaScript sandboxing with distortions; it's more compatible with standard libraries and allows cross-namespace component imports. LWS is now the default in most orgs.</p>"],
["lwc-014","Mid","concept","How do you call an LWC from a Quick Action or Flow?","<ul><li><b>Quick Action</b>: target <code>lightning__RecordAction</code> with <code>actionType</code> ScreenAction or Action (headless, uses <code>@api invoke()</code>). Close with <code>CloseActionScreenEvent</code>.</li>\n<li><b>Flow screen</b>: target <code>lightning__FlowScreen</code>, expose <code>@api</code> properties in targetConfig; use <code>FlowAttributeChangeEvent</code> to send values back and <code>FlowNavigationNextEvent</code> to move on.</li></ul>"],
["lwc-015","Basic","concept","How do you use Aura components and LWC together?","<p>Aura can contain LWC, but LWC cannot contain Aura. Communication: LWC dispatches events that Aura handles, Aura passes attributes down to LWC <code>@api</code> props, or both use Lightning Message Service.</p>"],
["lwc-016","Mid","concept","How do you write Jest tests for LWC?","<pre><code>import { createElement } from 'lwc';\nimport ContactList from 'c/contactList';\nimport getContacts from '@salesforce/apex/ContactCtrl.getContacts';\njest.mock('@salesforce/apex/ContactCtrl.getContacts',\n  () =&gt; { const { createApexTestWireAdapter } = require('@salesforce/sfdx-lwc-jest');\n          return { default: createApexTestWireAdapter(jest.fn()) }; }, { virtual: true });\n\nit('renders contacts', async () =&gt; {\n  const el = createElement('c-contact-list', { is: ContactList });\n  document.body.appendChild(el);\n  getContacts.emit([{ Id: '1', Name: 'Ann' }]);\n  await Promise.resolve();\n  expect(el.shadowRoot.querySelectorAll('li').length).toBe(1);\n});</code></pre>"],
["lwc-017","Senior","concept","How do you build a reusable, configurable LWC datatable for any object?","<ul><li>Expose <code>@api objectApiName</code>, <code>fieldSetName</code> or field list, filter, page size via targetConfigs.</li>\n<li>Apex reads the field set with <code>Schema</code> describe, builds columns (label, type), runs a dynamic query with <code>USER_MODE</code> and whitelisted fields.</li>\n<li>Use server-side pagination (OFFSET up to 2,000, or keyset pagination by Id for more).</li>\n<li>Support inline edit with <code>draft-values</code> and <code>updateRecord</code> or a bulk Apex save.</li></ul>"],
["lwc-018","Senior","concept","How do you handle errors consistently across LWC components?","<ul><li>A shared utility module <code>c/errorUtils</code> with <code>reduceErrors(error)</code> that normalises Apex, LDS (UI API) and JS errors into an array of messages.</li>\n<li>In Apex, throw <code>AuraHandledException</code> with a safe message (set via <code>setMessage</code>) and log the full detail server side.</li>\n<li>A base error panel component; use <code>errorCallback</code> as a boundary in container components.</li>\n<li>Show validation errors inline (<code>setCustomValidity</code> + <code>reportValidity</code>) and system errors via toast.</li></ul>"],
["lwc-019","Senior","concept","How do you build a parent LWC that dynamically renders different child components based on config?","<pre><code>&lt;!-- template --&gt;\n&lt;lwc:component lwc:is={componentCtor} record-id={recordId}&gt;&lt;/lwc:component&gt;\n\n// js\nasync connectedCallback() {\n  const cfg = await getConfig({ recordTypeId: this.rtId });   // Custom Metadata\n  const mod = await import(cfg.componentName);              // e.g. 'c/b2bSummary'\n  this.componentCtor = mod.default;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Requires <code>lightning__dynamicComponent</code> capability in meta.xml. Dynamic imports with a non-literal string must be statically analysable for packaging; list allowed components in a map.</p>"],
["lwc-020","Senior","concept","How do you subscribe to Platform Events or CDC in LWC?","<pre><code>import { subscribe, unsubscribe, onError } from 'lightning/empApi';\nchannel = '/event/Order_Status__e';   // CDC: '/data/AccountChangeEvent'\nasync connectedCallback() {\n  this.sub = await subscribe(this.channel, -1, (msg) =&gt; {\n    if (msg.data.payload.Order_Id__c === this.recordId) this.refresh();\n  });\n  onError((e) =&gt; console.error(e));\n}\ndisconnectedCallback() { unsubscribe(this.sub); }</code></pre>\n<p>empApi isn't supported in Experience Cloud sites or mobile. Replay -1 = new events only, -2 = all retained events (72 hours).</p>"]]);
SF.add("vf", [["vf-001","Basic","concept","What types of controllers exist in Visualforce?","<ul><li><b>Standard controller</b>: built-in CRUD for one object.</li>\n<li><b>Standard list controller</b>: <code>recordSetVar</code> for lists and pagination.</li>\n<li><b>Custom controller</b>: fully custom Apex, runs in system mode unless <code>with sharing</code>.</li>\n<li><b>Controller extension</b>: extends a standard/custom controller; constructor takes <code>ApexPages.StandardController</code>.</li></ul>"],
["vf-002","Mid","concept","What is view state and how do you reduce it?","<p>View state is the encrypted hidden form data holding controller state between postbacks. Limit 170 KB.</p>\n<ul><li>Mark non-essential variables <code>transient</code>.</li><li>Query only needed fields; avoid storing large collections.</li>\n<li>Use <code>&lt;apex:outputPanel&gt;</code> rerendering instead of full forms; use JavaScript Remoting (stateless).</li><li>Use a single <code>&lt;apex:form&gt;</code>.</li></ul>"],
["vf-003","Mid","concept","actionFunction vs actionSupport vs actionPoller vs JavaScript Remoting?","<ul><li><b>actionSupport</b>: adds AJAX to a component event (onchange).</li>\n<li><b>actionFunction</b>: generates a JS function that calls a controller method.</li>\n<li><b>actionPoller</b>: calls a method on a timer.</li>\n<li><b>JS Remoting</b> (<code>@RemoteAction</code> static method): no view state, faster, returns JSON to a callback. Best for performance.</li></ul>"],
["vf-004","Mid","concept","How do you render a Visualforce page as PDF?","<p><code>&lt;apex:page renderAs=\"pdf\"&gt;</code>. To attach or email: <code>Blob b = Page.InvoicePdf.getContentAsPDF();</code> then create a ContentVersion. Note <code>getContent()</code> counts as a callout and can't run in triggers; use a Queueable. CSS support is limited (CSS 2.1, no JS).</p>"],
["vf-005","Mid","concept","How do you use Visualforce in Lightning Experience?","<p>Enable \"Available for Lightning Experience\" on the page; it renders in an iframe. Use <code>lightningStylesheets=\"true\"</code> for SLDS styling. Use <code>sforce.one.navigateToSObject()</code> for navigation. Communicate with LWC via Lightning Message Service. You can also embed LWC in VF using Lightning Out (<code>$Lightning.use</code>).</p>"],
["vf-006","Senior","concept","What is your approach to migrating Visualforce pages to LWC?","<ul><li>Inventory pages with usage stats (Lightning Usage app, event monitoring) and retire unused ones.</li>\n<li>Replace simple forms with standard layouts, Dynamic Forms or Screen Flows.</li>\n<li>Reuse controller logic: move it into service classes exposed via <code>@AuraEnabled</code>.</li>\n<li>Keep PDF generation in VF (still the native option) and call it from LWC actions.</li></ul>"],
["vf-007","Basic","concept","How do you prevent XSS in Visualforce?","<p>Standard components (<code>apex:outputText</code>) escape by default; keep <code>escape=\"true\"</code>. When writing merge fields into JavaScript or HTML attributes, use <code>JSENCODE()</code>, <code>HTMLENCODE()</code>, <code>URLENCODE()</code>, <code>JSINHTMLENCODE()</code>. Never pass raw <code>{!$CurrentPage.parameters.x}</code> into script.</p>"],
["vf-008","Mid","concept","How do you pass parameters between Visualforce pages?","<p>Using a PageReference: <code>PageReference p = Page.Detail; p.getParameters().put('id', accId); p.setRedirect(true); return p;</code> Read with <code>ApexPages.currentPage().getParameters().get('id')</code>. With <code>setRedirect(false)</code> and the same controller, view state is preserved across pages (wizard pattern).</p>"]]);
SF.add("integ", [["integ-001","Basic","concept","What integration patterns does Salesforce describe?","<ul><li><b>Request and Reply</b>: synchronous callout, wait for response.</li>\n<li><b>Fire and Forget</b>: async; platform events, outbound messages, queueable callouts.</li>\n<li><b>Batch Data Synchronization</b>: ETL/Bulk API, CDC.</li>\n<li><b>Remote Call-In</b>: external system calls Salesforce REST/SOAP/custom APIs.</li>\n<li><b>UI Update Based on Data Changes</b>: streaming/empApi.</li>\n<li><b>Data Virtualization</b>: Salesforce Connect / external objects.</li></ul>"],
["integ-002","Basic","concept","How do you make a REST callout from Apex?","<pre><code>HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();\nreq.setEndpoint('callout:ERP_API/orders');   // Named Credential\nreq.setMethod('POST');\nreq.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');\nreq.setBody(JSON.serialize(payload));\nreq.setTimeout(20000);\nHttpResponse res = new Http().send(req);\nif (res.getStatusCode() == 200 || res.getStatusCode() == 201) {\n  OrderResponse r = (OrderResponse) JSON.deserialize(res.getBody(), OrderResponse.class);\n} else {\n  throw new CalloutException('ERP error ' + res.getStatusCode() + ': ' + res.getBody());\n}</code></pre>"],
["integ-003","Mid","concept","What are Named Credentials and External Credentials?","<p>The modern model separates them: an <b>External Credential</b> holds the authentication protocol (OAuth 2.0, JWT, Basic, Custom) and principals; a <b>Named Credential</b> holds the endpoint URL and references the external credential. Access is granted through permission sets mapped to principals.</p>\n<p>Benefits: no hard-coded secrets or Remote Site Settings, automatic token handling, per-user or named principal auth, merge fields in headers (<code>{!$Credential.ExtCred.apiKey}</code>).</p>"],
["integ-004","Mid","concept","How do you expose a custom REST API from Salesforce?","<pre><code>@RestResource(urlMapping='/orders/*')\nglobal with sharing class OrderApi {\n  @HttpGet\n  global static Order__c getOrder() {\n    String id = RestContext.request.requestURI.substringAfterLast('/');\n    return [SELECT Id, Name, Status__c FROM Order__c WHERE Id = :id WITH USER_MODE];\n  }\n  @HttpPost\n  global static Id create(String name, Decimal amount) {\n    Order__c o = new Order__c(Name = name, Amount__c = amount);\n    insert as user o;\n    return o.Id;\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Endpoint: <code>/services/apexrest/orders/</code>. Caller authenticates through a Connected App / External Client App with OAuth.</p>"],
["integ-005","Mid","concept","Which OAuth flows have you used and when?","<ul><li><b>Web Server (Authorization Code + PKCE)</b>: apps where a user logs in.</li>\n<li><b>JWT Bearer</b>: server-to-server without user interaction, using a certificate; common for middleware and CI/CD.</li>\n<li><b>Client Credentials</b>: server-to-server with a run-as integration user.</li>\n<li><b>Refresh Token</b>: keep sessions without re-login.</li>\n<li><b>Device flow</b>: CLI/IoT. Username-Password flow is discouraged.</li></ul>"],
["integ-006","Mid","concept","REST API vs SOAP API vs Bulk API 2.0 vs Composite API?","<ul><li><b>REST</b>: JSON, lightweight, mobile/web, record-level operations.</li>\n<li><b>SOAP</b>: WSDL-based, strongly typed (Enterprise/Partner WSDL), legacy enterprise systems.</li>\n<li><b>Bulk API 2.0</b>: async, large data loads (millions of rows), CSV.</li>\n<li><b>Composite / Composite Graph / sObject Collections</b>: multiple operations in one call (up to 25 subrequests, or 200 records in collections), reduces API calls, can be all-or-none.</li></ul>"],
["integ-007","Mid","concept","What is Change Data Capture and how is it different from Platform Events?","<p>CDC automatically publishes change events (create, update, delete, undelete) for selected objects, including changed fields in the header. You don't write publishing code. Platform Events are custom-defined and published by your code or flows. Use CDC to replicate Salesforce data to external systems; use Platform Events for business events with a custom payload.</p>"],
["integ-008","Mid","concept","How do you consume a SOAP service in Apex?","<p>Generate Apex classes from the WSDL in Setup (WSDL to Apex). Call the generated stub's methods; set endpoint to a Named Credential (<code>stub.endpoint_x = 'callout:SAP_WS'</code>). Test with <code>WebServiceMock</code> and <code>Test.setMock(WebServiceMock.class, ...)</code>. If WSDL has unsupported constructs, build the XML manually with <code>HttpRequest</code> and <code>Dom.Document</code>.</p>"],
["integ-009","Senior","concept","How do you handle callout failures and retries?","<ul><li>Do callouts in Queueable with a Finalizer to re-enqueue with exponential backoff and a retry counter.</li>\n<li>Persist failed payloads in an Integration_Log__c object with status for reprocessing (scheduled job picks up failures).</li>\n<li>Distinguish retryable (5xx, timeout) from non-retryable (4xx validation) errors.</li>\n<li>Make the target endpoint idempotent with an external id / idempotency key.</li>\n<li>Alert via email or Slack after N failures.</li></ul>"],
["integ-010","Mid","concept","What is the ‘You have uncommitted work pending’ error?","<p>Thrown when you do DML and then a callout in the same transaction. Order must be: callout first, then DML. Or move the callout into async (Queueable/future) after the DML.</p>"],
["integ-011","Mid","concept","What are External IDs and upsert used for in integrations?","<p>An External ID field stores the source system's key, is indexed and unique. <code>upsert records ExternalId__c;</code> inserts or updates without first querying for Salesforce Ids, and you can set lookups using the parent's external id (<code>new Contact(Account = new Account(ERP_Id__c = 'A-100'))</code>). This avoids duplicates and simplifies sync.</p>"],
["integ-012","Mid","concept","What is Salesforce Connect / External Objects?","<p>Shows external data in Salesforce in real time without copying it, via OData 2.0/4.0, cross-org, or custom Apex adapters. External objects end in <code>__x</code>, support lookups (external and indirect lookups), and can be queried with SOQL. Good when data is large and changes often; limits on callouts per hour and no triggers/reports in some cases.</p>"],
["integ-013","Senior","design","Design an integration between Salesforce and an ERP for orders.","<ul><li><b>Outbound</b>: on Order activation, publish Order_Activated__e. MuleSoft (or another middleware) subscribes via Pub/Sub API, transforms, posts to ERP. Decoupled and retryable.</li>\n<li><b>Inbound</b>: ERP sends status updates to middleware, which upserts by Order External ID through Composite API.</li>\n<li><b>Auth</b>: JWT Bearer with a dedicated integration user and permission set (API only, least privilege).</li>\n<li><b>Error handling</b>: dead-letter queue in middleware, Integration_Log__c in Salesforce, replay using event replay Ids.</li>\n<li><b>Volume</b>: Bulk API 2.0 for nightly reconciliation.</li></ul>"],
["integ-014","Mid","concept","How do you parse complex JSON in Apex?","<p>Typed: define wrapper classes and use <code>JSON.deserialize(body, Wrapper.class)</code> (handle reserved words like <code>currency</code> by renaming in the string or using <code>JSON.deserializeUntyped</code>). Untyped: <code>Map&lt;String,Object&gt; m = (Map&lt;String,Object&gt;) JSON.deserializeUntyped(body);</code> and cast nested values. For streaming large payloads, <code>JSONParser</code>.</p>"],
["integ-015","Basic","concept","What are the callout limits?","<p>100 callouts per transaction, 120 seconds total callout time (max timeout per callout 120 s), request/response size 6 MB sync and 12 MB async. Callouts aren't allowed after DML in the same transaction or directly from triggers.</p>"],
["integ-016","Mid","concept","What is an Outbound Message?","<p>A declarative SOAP message sent from a Flow or workflow rule to an endpoint with selected fields. Retries automatically for up to 24 hours, guaranteed delivery and ordering by record. Receiver must return an acknowledgment. Limited to SOAP and one object's fields; Platform Events/CDC are more modern alternatives.</p>"],
["integ-017","Senior","concept","How do you call an external API that needs a fresh OAuth token for each user?","<p>Use an External Credential with OAuth 2.0 Browser Flow and the principal type <b>Per User</b>. Each user authenticates once (Settings → External Credentials), Salesforce stores and refreshes their token. The Named Credential is referenced as <code>callout:Partner_API</code>, and the platform injects the right user's token. No token handling code, no custom storage of secrets.</p>"],
["integ-018","Senior","scenario","An external system calls your REST API 1M times a day and hits API limits. What do you do?","<ul><li>Check the call pattern: single-record calls → batch them with sObject Collections or Composite (200 records per call).</li>\n<li>Large loads → Bulk API 2.0 (separate batch limits).</li>\n<li>Polling for changes → switch to CDC / Platform Events via Pub/Sub API (push, not pull).</li>\n<li>Cache reference data on the client side.</li>\n<li>Review the org's 24-hour API allocation and buy add-on capacity if the volume is legitimate.</li>\n<li>Monitor with the API Usage notification and Event Monitoring API logs.</li></ul>"],
["integ-019","Senior","concept","How do you guarantee ordering and no duplicates when syncing from Salesforce to an external system?","<ul><li>Use CDC: events include <code>ChangeEventHeader.commitNumber</code> and <code>sequenceNumber</code> for ordering per transaction; the subscriber stores the last replay Id.</li>\n<li>Make the consumer idempotent: upsert by Salesforce Id and compare <code>commitTimestamp</code> or a version field (<code>SystemModstamp</code>) so an older event never overwrites newer data.</li>\n<li>Handle gap events and overflow events by re-querying the record.</li>\n<li>Persist replay position in middleware to resume after outages (events retained 72 hours).</li></ul>"]]);
SF.add("sec", [["sec-001","Basic","concept","Explain the Salesforce security model layers.","<ul><li><b>Org level</b>: login IP ranges, login hours, MFA, session settings.</li>\n<li><b>Object level</b>: CRUD via profiles and permission sets.</li>\n<li><b>Field level</b>: FLS via profiles and permission sets.</li>\n<li><b>Record level</b>: OWD, role hierarchy, sharing rules, teams, manual sharing, Apex sharing, territory.</li></ul>"],
["sec-002","Basic","concept","Profile vs Permission Set vs Permission Set Group?","<p>A user has exactly one profile (now mainly defaults: login hours/IPs, page layouts, default record types). Permission sets grant additional access and can be assigned to many users. Permission Set Groups bundle permission sets for a persona and support muting permission sets to remove specific permissions. Salesforce recommends minimum-access profiles plus permission sets.</p>"],
["sec-003","Mid","concept","How do OWD, role hierarchy and sharing rules interact?","<p>OWD sets the most restrictive baseline (Private, Public Read Only, Public Read/Write, Controlled by Parent). Role hierarchy then opens access upward (managers see subordinates' records, can be disabled for custom objects). Sharing rules open access laterally by owner or criteria. Access can only be opened, never restricted below OWD (except restriction rules).</p>"],
["sec-004","Mid","concept","What are Restriction Rules and Scoping Rules?","<p><b>Restriction rules</b> limit which records a user can see even if sharing grants access (e.g. contractors only see Cases where Type = 'Field'). <b>Scoping rules</b> set the default records users see in list views and searches without removing access.</p>"],
["sec-005","Mid","concept","How do you share records programmatically (Apex managed sharing)?","<pre><code>Project__Share s = new Project__Share();\ns.ParentId = projectId;\ns.UserOrGroupId = userId;\ns.AccessLevel = 'Edit';\ns.RowCause = Schema.Project__Share.RowCause.Team_Member__c; // Apex sharing reason\ninsert s;</code></pre>\n<p>Custom Apex sharing reasons keep shares when the owner changes (manual shares with RowCause Manual are deleted on owner change). Only for objects with OWD Private or Public Read Only.</p>"],
["sec-006","Mid","concept","Does Apex respect FLS and CRUD by default?","<p>No. Apex runs in system mode: it ignores CRUD and FLS, and sharing depends on <code>with/without sharing</code>. Enforce with <code>WITH USER_MODE</code>, <code>insert as user</code>, <code>Security.stripInaccessible()</code>, or describe checks (<code>Schema.sObjectType.Account.fields.Phone.isAccessible()</code>). AppExchange security review checks this.</p>"],
["sec-007","Mid","concept","What are common security vulnerabilities in Salesforce code?","<ul><li>SOQL injection (string concatenation of user input).</li><li>XSS in VF/Aura when outputting unescaped data.</li>\n<li>CRUD/FLS violations in <code>@AuraEnabled</code> methods.</li><li>Hard-coded credentials or Ids.</li>\n<li>Insecure <code>without sharing</code> in classes exposed to communities/guest users.</li><li>Open redirects using URL parameters.</li></ul>\n<p>Tools: Salesforce Code Analyzer (PMD, ESLint, Graph Engine), Checkmarx portal.</p>"],
["sec-008","Mid","concept","What is Shield and what does it include?","<ul><li><b>Platform Encryption</b>: encryption at rest for fields/files with tenant secrets, deterministic encryption for filtering.</li>\n<li><b>Event Monitoring</b>: log files of logins, report exports, API calls, page views; Transaction Security policies to block actions.</li>\n<li><b>Field Audit Trail</b>: retain field history up to 10 years.</li>\n<li><b>Data Detect</b>: find sensitive data.</li></ul>"],
["sec-009","Basic","concept","What are Custom Permissions and how do you check them?","<p>Feature-level flags assigned via permission sets. Check in Apex with <code>FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Approve_Discounts')</code>, in formulas/validation rules with <code>$Permission.Approve_Discounts</code>, and in LWC with <code>import hasPerm from '@salesforce/customPermission/Approve_Discounts';</code>.</p>"],
["sec-010","Mid","concept","What is ‘Implicit sharing’?","<p>Built-in sharing that isn't configured: parent implicit (access to a Contact/Opportunity/Case gives Read access to the parent Account), child implicit (Account owner gets access to child records based on role settings), and portal/community implicit sharing. It can't be turned off.</p>"],
["sec-011","Senior","scenario","A user can't see a record. How do you troubleshoot?","<ul><li>Use <b>\"Sharing\" / \"View Sharing Hierarchy\"</b> on the record or query <code>UserRecordAccess</code>.</li>\n<li>Check object CRUD on profile/permission sets and FLS for fields shown.</li>\n<li>Check OWD, owner's role, sharing rules, team membership, territory assignment.</li>\n<li>Check restriction rules and record type / page layout assignment.</li>\n<li>Check if it's a list view filter or scoping rule rather than access.</li>\n<li>Log in as the user (with permission) to reproduce.</li></ul>"],
["sec-012","Mid","concept","How do you secure a Connected App / integration user?","<ul><li>Use the \"Salesforce Integration\" user license with API-only permission set.</li>\n<li>Admin-approved users only, IP relaxation off, minimal OAuth scopes.</li>\n<li>JWT/certificate auth instead of passwords; rotate certificates.</li>\n<li>Session timeout and refresh token policy; monitor with Event Monitoring.</li></ul>"],
["sec-013","Senior","scenario","An @AuraEnabled method used on an Experience Cloud site returns data the external user shouldn't see. How did it happen and how do you fix it?","<p>Likely causes: the class is <code>without sharing</code> or has no sharing keyword (and is called from a context that defaults to system), the query has no user-mode enforcement, or it accepts an Id parameter without checking the user can access it (IDOR).</p>\n<ul><li>Use <code>with sharing</code> / <code>inherited sharing</code> and <code>WITH USER_MODE</code>.</li>\n<li>Never trust Ids from the client; filter by the user's context (<code>WHERE AccountId = :currentUser.Contact.AccountId</code>).</li>\n<li>Restrict Apex class access by profile/permission set for the community profile.</li>\n<li>Review sharing sets and guest user sharing rules; run Code Analyzer and the Experience Cloud security checks.</li></ul>"],
["sec-014","Senior","concept","How do you implement field-level masking for some users but not others (e.g. PAN or Aadhaar numbers)?","<ul><li>Shield Platform Encryption protects data at rest but doesn't mask it on screen. Use FLS to hide the full field from most users and give them a formula field with a masked value (<code>'XXXX-' &amp; RIGHT(PAN__c, 4)</code>).</li>\n<li>In Apex, return the masked value via <code>stripInaccessible</code> and a DTO.</li>\n<li>Data Mask for sandboxes, so real numbers never appear outside production.</li>\n<li>Event Monitoring + Transaction Security policy to alert on bulk exports of the field.</li></ul>"]]);
SF.add("admin", [["admin-001","Basic","concept","Lookup vs Master-Detail relationship?","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th></th><th>Lookup</th><th>Master-Detail</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Required parent</td><td>Optional</td><td>Required</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cascade delete</td><td>No (configurable for some)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sharing</td><td>Independent</td><td>Child inherits parent (Controlled by Parent)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Roll-up summary</td><td>No (use Flow/Apex/DLRS)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Owner field on child</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Limit per object</td><td>40 total</td><td>2</td></tr></table></div>"],
["admin-002","Basic","concept","What is a junction object?","<p>A custom object with two master-detail relationships that models a many-to-many relationship, e.g. Job_Application__c linking Candidate and Job_Position. The first master-detail is the primary (controls look and feel, ownership).</p>"],
["admin-003","Mid","concept","What types of Flows exist and when do you use each?","<ul><li><b>Screen flow</b>: guided UI.</li><li><b>Record-triggered</b>: before-save (fast field updates) and after-save (related records, actions), plus async path for callouts.</li>\n<li><b>Schedule-triggered</b>: batch on a schedule.</li><li><b>Platform event-triggered</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Autolaunched</b>: called from Apex, REST, other flows, buttons.</li><li><b>Orchestration</b>: multi-step, multi-user processes.</li></ul>\n<p>Workflow rules and Process Builder are retired; migrate with the Migrate to Flow tool.</p>"],
["admin-004","Mid","concept","How do you call Apex from a Flow?","<pre><code>public class CreditCheck {\n  @InvocableMethod(label='Run Credit Check' category='Finance')\n  public static List&lt;Result&gt; run(List&lt;Request&gt; reqs) {\n    List&lt;Result&gt; out = new List&lt;Result&gt;();\n    for (Request r : reqs) { Result x = new Result(); x.score = 700; out.add(x); }\n    return out;\n  }\n  public class Request { @InvocableVariable(required=true) public Id accountId; }\n  public class Result  { @InvocableVariable public Integer score; }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Must be bulk-safe: Flow batches interviews, so the method receives a list.</p>"],
["admin-005","Basic","concept","What is a validation rule? Give an example.","<p>A formula that returns TRUE to block the save with an error message. Example: close date can't be in the past for open opportunities:</p>\n<pre><code>AND( NOT(IsClosed), CloseDate &lt; TODAY(), NOT($Permission.Bypass_Validation) )</code></pre>"],
["admin-006","Mid","concept","Formula field vs Roll-up summary vs Flow for calculations?","<p>Formula: calculated on read, no storage, can reference parents (up to 10 levels) but not children. Roll-up summary: COUNT/SUM/MIN/MAX of master-detail children, stored. Flow/Apex: needed for lookup rollups or complex logic; stored value, but you must handle all change paths (insert, update, delete, reparent, undelete).</p>"],
["admin-007","Mid","concept","How do you deploy changes between orgs?","<ul><li>Change Sets (simple, no version control, org-to-org connected).</li>\n<li>Salesforce CLI with source tracking + Git, deploying via CI/CD (GitHub Actions, Jenkins, Copado, Gearset).</li>\n<li>Unlocked packages for modular delivery.</li>\n<li>DevOps Center for admin-friendly Git-based workflow.</li></ul>\n<p>Use <code>sf project deploy start --dry-run --test-level RunLocalTests</code> to validate; quick deploy within 10 days.</p>"],
["admin-008","Basic","concept","What sandbox types are there?","<ul><li><b>Developer</b>: metadata only, 200 MB data, refresh daily.</li><li><b>Developer Pro</b>: metadata, 1 GB, daily.</li>\n<li><b>Partial Copy</b>: metadata + sample data via template, 5 GB, every 5 days.</li><li><b>Full</b>: full copy of data, refresh every 29 days; for UAT and performance testing.</li></ul>"],
["admin-009","Mid","concept","What are Record Types and Page Layouts used for?","<p>Record types provide different business processes, picklist values and page layouts for the same object (e.g. B2B vs B2C Opportunity). Page layouts control field placement, related lists and buttons; assignment is by profile and record type. Dynamic Forms and Lightning Record Pages add visibility rules on top.</p>"],
["admin-010","Mid","concept","What are duplicate and matching rules?","<p>Matching rules define how records are compared (exact/fuzzy on fields). Duplicate rules decide what happens: allow with alert or block, and whether to report. In Apex, handle <code>DuplicateError</code> in SaveResult and use <code>Database.DMLOptions.DuplicateRuleHeader.allowSave</code> to override when appropriate.</p>"],
["admin-011","Mid","concept","How do approval processes work and can you start one from Apex?","<p>Define entry criteria, approval steps, approvers, and actions (initial, approval, rejection, recall). Records are locked during approval. From Apex:</p>\n<pre><code>Approval.ProcessSubmitRequest req = new Approval.ProcessSubmitRequest();\nreq.setObjectId(oppId);\nreq.setProcessDefinitionNameOrId('Discount_Approval');\nApproval.ProcessResult r = Approval.process(req);</code></pre>"],
["admin-012","Basic","concept","How do you track field changes?","<p>Enable field history tracking (up to 20 fields per object, retained 18 months; 10 years with Field Audit Trail). Query via <code>AccountHistory</code> or <code>ObjectName__History</code>. Setup Audit Trail tracks config changes for 180 days.</p>"]]);
SF.add("sales", [["sales-001","Basic","concept","Explain the lead conversion process.","<p>Converting a Lead creates an Account, Contact and optionally an Opportunity (or links to existing ones). Custom lead fields map to target objects via Lead Field Mapping. In Apex: <code>Database.LeadConvert lc = new Database.LeadConvert(); lc.setLeadId(id); lc.setConvertedStatus('Qualified'); Database.convertLead(lc);</code> Converted leads become read-only.</p>"],
["sales-002","Mid","concept","How do Opportunity Products, Price Books and Schedules relate?","<p>Product2 is the catalog item. PricebookEntry links a product to a price book with a price (a Standard Price Book entry must exist first). OpportunityLineItem references a PricebookEntry; the Opportunity's Pricebook2Id must match. Amount on Opportunity becomes the sum of line items. Product schedules split quantity/revenue over time.</p>"],
["sales-003","Mid","concept","How does Collaborative Forecasting work?","<p>Forecasts roll up by role hierarchy (or territory) using forecast types (Opportunity Revenue, Quantity, Product family, Splits, custom measures). Forecast categories (Pipeline, Best Case, Commit, Closed, Omitted) map from Opportunity stages. Managers can adjust subordinates' numbers; quotas are loaded via ForecastingQuota.</p>"],
["sales-004","Mid","concept","What is Enterprise Territory Management?","<p>Assigns accounts to territories by rules (e.g. Billing Country + Industry), independent of role hierarchy. Territory models (planning/active), territory types and hierarchy. Users assigned to territories get account access based on territory-level settings. Opportunities can be assigned via a filter-based Apex class implementing <code>TerritoryMgmt.OpportunityTerritory2AssignmentFilter</code>.</p>"],
["sales-005","Mid","concept","What are Opportunity Splits and Teams?","<p>Opportunity Teams add members with roles and access levels. Splits divide credit: revenue splits must total 100%; overlay splits can be any value. Used in forecasting and commission calculations.</p>"],
["sales-006","Senior","concept","How have you customised Sales Cloud with code?","<ul><li>Auto-assignment of opportunities to territories with Apex filter class.</li>\n<li>Discount approval with validation on OLI + approval process + LWC quote builder.</li>\n<li>Lead routing with round-robin using custom metadata and a queue-based Apex service.</li>\n<li>Integration with ERP for product/price sync via Bulk API and external ids.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"tip\">Have one real example ready with numbers (volume, users, what improved).</p>"]]);
SF.add("service", [["service-001","Basic","concept","What are the ways Cases get created?","<p>Email-to-Case (On-Demand), Web-to-Case (500 per day limit), Messaging/Chat (Messaging for In-App and Web), phone via Service Cloud Voice, Experience Cloud self-service, API, and manual entry. Assignment rules and auto-response rules then route and acknowledge.</p>"],
["service-002","Mid","concept","Explain Omni-Channel routing.","<p>Omni-Channel pushes work items (cases, chats, leads) to agents based on availability and capacity. Components: Service Channels (object), Routing Configurations (priority, model: least active / most available, units of capacity), Queues or Skills-based routing, Presence Statuses and Presence Configurations. Omni-Channel Flows let you route with flow logic (skills, attributes, bots).</p>"],
["service-003","Mid","concept","What are Entitlements and Milestones?","<p>Entitlements define support terms a customer has (e.g. Premium support). Entitlement processes contain milestones (First Response in 1 hour, Resolution in 8 hours) with warning, violation and success actions. Business hours apply. Milestone timers show on the case; <code>CaseMilestone.CompletionDate</code> can be set in Apex when a criteria is met.</p>"],
["service-004","Mid","concept","How does Salesforce Knowledge work?","<p>Lightning Knowledge uses the Knowledge__kav object with record types for article types. Articles have versions and publication statuses (Draft, Published, Archived), data categories for visibility, and channels (internal, customer, partner, public). Agents attach articles to cases; articles surface in Experience Cloud and Einstein search.</p>"],
["service-005","Mid","concept","What is the Service Console and how do you customise it?","<p>A console app with workspace tabs and subtabs, utility bar (Omni, macros, history), split view. Customise with Lightning App Builder, Macros, Quick Text, and LWC using <code>lightning/platformWorkspaceApi</code> to open tabs, set labels and refresh.</p>"],
["service-006","Mid","concept","How does Email-to-Case threading work?","<p>Newer orgs use Lightning threading tokens in email headers (and optionally in subject/body) to match replies to the existing Case. Legacy ref Ids in subject/body are retired. In Apex you can use <code>EmailMessages.getRecordIdFromEmail(subject, text, html)</code> for custom inbound email handlers.</p>"],
["service-007","Senior","design","Design an escalation process for high-priority cases.","<ul><li>Entitlement process with milestones by priority and business hours.</li>\n<li>Violation actions: notify manager, re-route via Omni to a Tier 2 queue/skill.</li>\n<li>Escalation rules as a fallback for time-based reassignment.</li>\n<li>Slack/Teams notification via Flow or Platform Event subscriber.</li>\n<li>Dashboard for SLA compliance using CaseMilestone reports.</li></ul>"],
["service-008","Senior","concept","How would you implement skills-based routing where a Case needs both a language and a product skill?","<ul><li>Enable Skills-Based Routing in Omni-Channel; create Skills (Hindi, English, Product_A) and assign Service Resources with levels.</li>\n<li>Use an Omni-Channel Flow (or Apex) to create a <code>PendingServiceRouting</code> with <code>IsReadyForRouting = false</code>, add <code>SkillRequirement</code> records (language and product, optional \"drop after N seconds\" for secondary skills), then set it ready.</li>\n<li>Set fallback queue and timeouts so cases don't wait forever.</li>\n<li>Monitor with Omni Supervisor.</li></ul>"]]);
SF.add("exp", [["exp-001","Basic","concept","What are Experience Cloud licenses and when do you use each?","<ul><li><b>Customer Community</b>: high-volume B2C, cases and knowledge; uses sharing sets, no roles.</li>\n<li><b>Customer Community Plus</b>: roles and sharing rules, reports, more objects.</li>\n<li><b>Partner Community</b>: PRM, leads, opportunities, deal registration.</li>\n<li><b>External Apps</b>: custom app-like experiences.</li>\n<li>Also login-based vs member-based licensing.</li></ul>"],
["exp-002","Mid","concept","Aura vs LWR sites?","<p>Aura sites (Customer Service template) support Aura and LWC components and more out-of-the-box components. LWR (Lightning Web Runtime) sites (Build Your Own LWR) are faster with static publishing and CDN caching, support only LWC, and changes require republishing. Some features (ShowToastEvent, some standard components) behave differently in LWR.</p>"],
["exp-003","Mid","concept","What are Sharing Sets and Share Groups?","<p><b>Sharing Sets</b> give high-volume external users (Customer Community) access to records related to their Account or Contact, e.g. all Cases where Case.AccountId = User.Contact.AccountId. <b>Share Groups</b> share records owned by those high-volume users with internal users. Plus licenses can use role-based sharing rules instead.</p>"],
["exp-004","Mid","concept","How do you secure guest user access?","<ul><li>Guest users have OWD set to Private for all objects, can't be in roles, and records they create are owned by a default internal owner.</li>\n<li>Use guest user sharing rules (read-only) for specific records.</li>\n<li>Apex for guests must be <code>with sharing</code> and enforce FLS; avoid exposing <code>without sharing</code> <code>@AuraEnabled</code> methods.</li>\n<li>Review the guest profile regularly (Guest User Access Report).</li></ul>"],
["exp-005","Mid","concept","How do you customise an Experience Cloud site with code?","<p>Build LWCs with target <code>lightningCommunity__Page</code> and <code>lightningCommunity__Default</code>, use <code>@salesforce/community/Id</code> and <code>basePath</code>, custom theme layouts, CSS overrides through branding/head markup, and navigation via NavigationMixin with <code>comm__namedPage</code>. Apex can check <code>Network.getNetworkId()</code> to behave differently on sites.</p>"],
["exp-006","Senior","concept","How do you enable a partner portal for deal registration?","<ul><li>Enable partner accounts, create partner users with Partner Community license and roles (Executive/Manager/User).</li>\n<li>Lead assignment and sharing to partner roles; Deal registration flow converts leads to opportunities.</li>\n<li>Channel manager sees partner pipeline via role hierarchy above partner roles.</li>\n<li>Partner central template, MDF and channel programs if PRM add-ons are in use.</li></ul>"],
["exp-007","Senior","scenario","External users of a high-volume Customer Community need to see Cases from all Contacts of their Account. How?","<p>High-volume licenses don't use roles, so sharing rules don't apply. Create a <b>Sharing Set</b> on the community profile: Case.AccountId matches User.Contact.AccountId, access Read/Write. For records owned by these users to be visible to internal users, use <b>Share Groups</b>. If more complex logic is needed (e.g. parent-account hierarchies), consider Customer Community Plus with Account role or Apex managed sharing on a custom object.</p>"]]);
SF.add("scenario", [["scenario-001","Senior","scenario","A batch job fails with ‘Apex CPU time limit exceeded’. What do you do?","<ul><li>Reduce scope size (e.g. 200 → 50) and test.</li>\n<li>Profile with debug logs / Apex Log Analyzer to find hot spots: nested loops, repeated describe calls, string concatenation in loops.</li>\n<li>Replace nested loops with Maps (O(n) instead of O(n²)).</li>\n<li>Check what triggers and flows fire on the updated records; bypass them for the batch user if appropriate.</li>\n<li>Move independent work into separate jobs.</li></ul>"],
["scenario-002","Senior","scenario","Users report a record page loads slowly. How do you investigate?","<ul><li>Lightning Usage App / Experienced Page Time (EPT) and Chrome DevTools.</li>\n<li>Count components on the page; move rarely used ones to tabs (tabs render lazily).</li>\n<li>Look for multiple components calling the same Apex; share via LDS or cacheable methods.</li>\n<li>Check related lists with heavy rollups, record page with too many fields (Dynamic Forms with visibility).</li>\n<li>Run Salesforce Optimizer.</li></ul>"],
["scenario-003","Senior","scenario","How do you handle ‘UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW’ errors?","<p>Two transactions try to update the same record (often a shared parent Account during child updates or rollups). Fixes: order records by parent Id in batches, reduce batch size, use <code>FOR UPDATE</code> to lock deliberately, avoid data skew (more than 10,000 children per parent or owner), use serial mode in Bulk API, and retry with backoff.</p>"],
["scenario-004","Senior","scenario","What is data skew and why does it matter?","<ul><li><b>Account data skew</b>: >10k children under one account causes lock contention and slow sharing recalculation.</li>\n<li><b>Ownership skew</b>: one user owns >10k records of an object; role changes trigger heavy sharing recalculation. Put that user in a role without a deep hierarchy or no role.</li>\n<li><b>Lookup skew</b>: many records point to one lookup record, causing locks.</li></ul>"],
["scenario-005","Mid","design","How would you design a solution to auto-close Cases inactive for 7 days?","<p>Schedule-triggered Flow or Schedulable + Batch: query Cases where Status = 'Waiting on Customer' and LastModifiedDate &lt; LAST_N_DAYS:7, send a reminder at day 5, close at day 7. Add a custom field Last_Customer_Response__c updated by Email-to-Case so system updates don't reset the timer. Batch is better for high volume.</p>"],
["scenario-006","Senior","scenario","How do you structure a release for a team of 8 developers?","<ul><li>Git with feature branches, pull requests, code review and static analysis (Code Analyzer) as checks.</li>\n<li>Scratch orgs or developer sandboxes per developer; integration sandbox (dev → QA → UAT → prod).</li>\n<li>CI validates against QA with RunLocalTests on every PR; deploy merges automatically.</li>\n<li>Destructive changes managed explicitly; data/config (custom metadata) in source.</li>\n<li>Release notes and rollback plan; post-deploy manual steps documented.</li></ul>"],
["scenario-007","Mid","scenario","How do you debug a production issue you can't reproduce in sandbox?","<ul><li>Set a trace flag on the affected user (Debug Logs) and ask them to reproduce.</li>\n<li>Check your custom error log object / Platform Event logs.</li>\n<li>Compare data and config differences (sharing, record types, validation rules, permission sets).</li>\n<li>Check Apex exception emails and Setup → Apex Jobs for async failures.</li>\n<li>Refresh a partial/full sandbox with relevant data if needed.</li></ul>"],
["scenario-008","Senior","scenario","Tell me about the most complex solution you built.","<p>Use STAR (Situation, Task, Action, Result). Mention the business problem, data volume, your design choices and trade-offs (why Queueable over Batch, why Platform Events), how you handled limits/security/testing, and the measurable outcome (e.g. reduced sync failures from 8% to under 0.5%, cut manual effort 20 hours/week).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\">Interviewers at 5 years expect you to talk about trade-offs and what you would do differently.</p>"],
["scenario-009","Mid","scenario","How would you handle a requirement to show Account data from two Salesforce orgs?","<p>Options: Salesforce Connect cross-org adapter (real-time, external objects), Salesforce to Salesforce (legacy), or REST integration with Named Credentials to the other org via OAuth JWT. Choose based on volume, need for reporting (copy data) vs real-time view (virtualize).</p>"],
["scenario-010","Senior","scenario","When would you NOT write code?","<p>When a declarative feature fits: before-save Flow for field updates, validation rules, Dynamic Forms, standard Lightning components, duplicate rules, approval processes, assignment rules. Code adds maintenance and test cost; choose it for complex logic, high volume, integrations, or UI that standard components can't deliver. Being able to explain this balance is a sign of seniority.</p>"],
["scenario-011","Mid","scenario","How do you implement round-robin case assignment?","<ul><li>Custom Metadata or object holds agent list with last-assigned index (or use Omni-Channel which handles this natively — prefer it).</li>\n<li>If custom: in a before-insert handler, pull eligible agents, use a counter field on a Custom Setting with <code>FOR UPDATE</code> to avoid two transactions assigning the same index.</li>\n<li>Respect availability (out-of-office, capacity).</li></ul>"],
["scenario-012","Senior","scenario","What has changed in Salesforce development recently that you keep up with?","<p>Good topics: Agentforce and Prompt Builder (AI agents calling Apex/Flow actions), Lightning Web Security, <code>USER_MODE</code> database operations, Named/External Credentials redesign, Flow replacing Workflow/Process Builder, LWR sites, Pub/Sub API, Data Cloud integration, External Client Apps replacing Connected Apps for new integrations, Salesforce Code Analyzer v5.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\">Mention release notes, Trailhead and Salesforce Developers blog as how you keep current.</p>"],
["scenario-013","Senior","design","Design a data archival strategy for an org with 80 million Case records.","<ul><li>Define retention policy with the business (e.g. keep 2 years online).</li>\n<li>Options: Big Objects (store in Salesforce, query with limited SOQL on index fields), external storage (data lake / Data Cloud) with Salesforce Connect for on-demand viewing, or a managed archiving tool (Own, Odaseva).</li>\n<li>Move in batches using Bulk API with hard delete permission and emptied recycle bin.</li>\n<li>Preserve attachments (ContentDocument links) and audit requirements.</li>\n<li>Benefits: faster queries/reports, lower storage cost, less lock and sharing recalculation load.</li></ul>"],
["scenario-014","Senior","scenario","Your org has 60 percent code coverage overall but a deployment fails. Why, and how do you fix it long-term?","<p>Deployment requires 75% org-wide coverage when running tests, every trigger needs coverage, and all tests must pass. With RunLocalTests a single failing test (often due to new validation rules, required fields or data assumptions) fails the deployment.</p>\n<ul><li>Use a test data factory so tests adapt when required fields change.</li>\n<li>Remove tests with <code>SeeAllData=true</code>.</li>\n<li>Add meaningful assertions, not coverage-only tests.</li>\n<li>Run tests in CI on every pull request so failures appear early.</li>\n<li>Use RunSpecifiedTests for targeted deployments where allowed (each class in the package needs 75%).</li></ul>"],
["scenario-015","Senior","scenario","How would you migrate 2 million Accounts and related Contacts from a legacy CRM with zero downtime?","<ul><li>Map data, create External Id fields for legacy keys on each object.</li>\n<li>Bypass automations for the migration user (custom permission checked by triggers, flows and validation rules).</li>\n<li>Load order: Users → Accounts → Contacts → Opportunities → Activities; use upsert with External Ids for relationships.</li>\n<li>Bulk API 2.0, parallel mode where no lock contention; serial or sorted by parent for child objects to avoid locks.</li>\n<li>Defer sharing calculations during load (Support enables it), then recalculate.</li>\n<li>Run a full trial load in a Full sandbox, reconcile counts and sample records, then delta load at cut-over.</li></ul>"]]);
SF.add("soql", [["soql-001","Basic","code","Write a parent-to-child and child-to-parent SOQL query.","<pre><code>// Child to parent: dot notation, up to 5 levels\nSELECT Id, LastName, Account.Name, Account.Owner.Email FROM Contact\n\n// Parent to child: subquery on the child relationship name (plural, __r for custom)\nSELECT Id, Name,\n  (SELECT Id, LastName FROM Contacts),\n  (SELECT Id, Amount FROM Opportunities WHERE IsClosed = false)\nFROM Account WHERE Industry = 'Banking'</code></pre>\n<p>Custom relationship: <code>Project__r.Name</code> (child → parent) and <code>(SELECT Id FROM Tasks__r)</code> (parent → child). Since API v58 parent-to-child nesting goes up to 5 levels in Apex.</p>"],
["soql-002","Mid","concept","What is a semi-join and anti-join? Give examples.","<pre><code>// Semi-join: Accounts that HAVE an open Opportunity\nSELECT Id, Name FROM Account\nWHERE Id IN (SELECT AccountId FROM Opportunity WHERE IsClosed = false)\n\n// Anti-join: Accounts with NO Contacts\nSELECT Id, Name FROM Account\nWHERE Id NOT IN (SELECT AccountId FROM Contact)</code></pre>\n<p>Rules: the subquery must select an Id or reference field, max 2 subqueries per WHERE, no nested semi-joins, and <code>NOT IN</code> subqueries can't be used with OR.</p>"],
["soql-003","Mid","code","Write aggregate queries with GROUP BY, HAVING and ROLLUP.","<pre><code>// Accounts with more than 5 closed-won deals and their total\nSELECT AccountId, COUNT(Id) deals, SUM(Amount) total\nFROM Opportunity\nWHERE StageName = 'Closed Won' AND CloseDate = THIS_FISCAL_YEAR\nGROUP BY AccountId\nHAVING COUNT(Id) &gt; 5\nORDER BY SUM(Amount) DESC\n\n// Subtotals per stage and owner\nSELECT StageName, OwnerId, SUM(Amount) FROM Opportunity\nGROUP BY ROLLUP(StageName, OwnerId)</code></pre>\n<p>Read results via <code>AggregateResult.get('total')</code>. Every aggregated row counts as one row toward the 50,000 limit only after grouping, but all underlying records still count toward query rows in some cases; <code>COUNT()</code> without a field returns an Integer.</p>"],
["soql-004","Senior","concept","What makes a SOQL query selective and how do you fix a non-selective query?","<p>On objects with more than 200,000 records, triggers throw <code>Non-selective query against large object type</code> when the optimizer would do a full table scan. A filter is selective when it uses an index and returns under the threshold:</p>\n<ul><li>Standard index: under 30% of the first million records, 15% after, capped at 1 million rows.</li>\n<li>Custom index: under 10% of the first million, 5% after, capped at 333,333 rows.</li></ul>\n<p>Indexed by default: Id, Name, OwnerId, CreatedDate, SystemModstamp, RecordTypeId, lookups/master-detail, External Id and unique fields.</p>\n<p>Non-selective patterns: <code>!=</code>, <code>NOT LIKE</code>, leading wildcard <code>LIKE '%abc'</code>, comparing to null, formula fields that aren't deterministic, OR across non-indexed fields.</p>\n<p>Fixes: add an indexed filter (date range, record type), request a custom index from Support, use External Id fields, check the Query Plan tool in Developer Console (cost below 1 is selective).</p>"],
["soql-005","Mid","concept","How do you query records across a date range using date literals?","<pre><code>SELECT Id FROM Case WHERE CreatedDate = LAST_N_DAYS:30\nSELECT Id FROM Opportunity WHERE CloseDate = NEXT_QUARTER\nSELECT Id FROM Lead WHERE CreatedDate = THIS_WEEK AND HOUR_IN_DAY(CreatedDate) &gt; 18\nSELECT CALENDAR_MONTH(CloseDate) m, SUM(Amount) FROM Opportunity\n  WHERE CloseDate = THIS_YEAR GROUP BY CALENDAR_MONTH(CloseDate)</code></pre>\n<p>Datetime literals are in GMT unless you use <code>convertTimezone()</code> inside date functions. Bind a Date with <code>:startDate</code> when the range is dynamic.</p>"],
["soql-006","Mid","concept","What does FOR UPDATE do and when do you use it?","<p><code>SELECT Id, Counter__c FROM Sequence__c WHERE Name = 'Invoice' FOR UPDATE</code> locks the returned rows until the transaction ends, so a second transaction waits (up to ~10 seconds) instead of reading a stale value. Use it for counters, sequence generators and round-robin indexes. It can't be combined with ORDER BY in some cases and long locks cause <code>UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW</code>.</p>"],
["soql-007","Senior","concept","How do you paginate over more than 2,000 records?","<p><code>OFFSET</code> is capped at 2,000. Use keyset pagination instead: order by Id (or an indexed unique field) and pass the last Id of the previous page.</p>\n<pre><code>@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)\npublic static List&lt;Account&gt; nextPage(Id lastId, Integer size) {\n  String q = 'SELECT Id, Name FROM Account';\n  if (lastId != null) q += ' WHERE Id &gt; :lastId';\n  q += ' ORDER BY Id LIMIT :size';\n  return Database.queryWithBinds(q, new Map&lt;String,Object&gt;{\n    'lastId' =&gt; lastId, 'size' =&gt; size }, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>For server-side Visualforce, <code>StandardSetController</code> handles up to 10,000 records.</p>"],
["soql-008","Mid","concept","What are polymorphic relationships and TYPEOF?","<p>Fields like <code>Task.WhatId</code>, <code>Task.WhoId</code> and <code>Case.OwnerId</code> can point to several object types.</p>\n<pre><code>SELECT Id, Subject,\n  TYPEOF What\n    WHEN Account THEN Name, Industry\n    WHEN Opportunity THEN Name, StageName\n    ELSE Name\n  END\nFROM Task WHERE What.Type IN ('Account','Opportunity')</code></pre>\n<p>In Apex, check the type with <code>t.What instanceof Account</code> or <code>t.WhatId.getSObjectType() == Account.SObjectType</code>.</p>"],
["soql-009","Mid","concept","What are the SOSL syntax pieces and limits?","<pre><code>List&lt;List&lt;SObject&gt;&gt; r = [\n  FIND :term IN NAME FIELDS\n  RETURNING Account(Id, Name WHERE Industry = 'Banking' LIMIT 10),\n            Contact(Id, Name, Email),\n            Lead(Id, Name, Company WHERE IsConverted = false)\n  WITH SNIPPET LIMIT 50];\nList&lt;Account&gt; accs = (List&lt;Account&gt;) r[0];</code></pre>\n<ul><li>Search groups: ALL FIELDS, NAME FIELDS, EMAIL FIELDS, PHONE FIELDS, SIDEBAR FIELDS.</li>\n<li>Term needs at least 2 characters; wildcards <code>*</code> and <code>?</code>; operators AND, OR, AND NOT, quotes for phrases.</li>\n<li>20 SOSL per transaction, 2,000 rows max.</li>\n<li>Search index is updated asynchronously, so newly inserted records may not appear immediately. In tests use <code>Test.setFixedSearchResults(ids)</code>.</li></ul>"],
["soql-010","Senior","scenario","When would you choose SOSL over SOQL even for one object?","<p>When searching text across many fields or with partial words: SOQL <code>LIKE '%term%'</code> on a large table is non-selective and slow, while SOSL uses the search index. Example: an LWC \"find customer\" box matching name, email or phone on Contact. SOSL also handles stemming and tokens. Trade-off: index lag and less precise filtering, so you often follow SOSL with a SOQL query on the returned Ids.</p>"],
["soql-011","Mid","concept","What are the SOQL limits on query structure?","<ul><li>100 SOQL statements sync, 200 async; 50,000 rows retrieved.</li>\n<li>Query string max 100,000 characters; WHERE clause max 4,000 characters (for some filters).</li>\n<li>Max 55 child-to-parent relationships and 20 parent-to-child subqueries per query.</li>\n<li>A subquery's child rows count toward the row limit; a parent with many children can hit <code>Aggregate query has too many rows for direct assignment</code> — loop the child list with a for loop instead of assigning it.</li></ul>"],
["soql-012","Mid","concept","What is the difference between a SOQL for loop and a list query?","<pre><code>// List: loads all rows into heap at once\nList&lt;Contact&gt; all = [SELECT Id FROM Contact];\n\n// SOQL for loop: fetched in batches of 200 using queryMore\nfor (List&lt;Contact&gt; chunk : [SELECT Id, Email FROM Contact WHERE Email = null]) {\n  // process 200 at a time\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The for-loop form keeps heap low for large result sets, but the 50,000 row limit still applies. Doing DML inside the chunked loop is acceptable only if the number of chunks stays below 150.</p>"],
["soql-013","Senior","concept","How would you query the Top 3 Opportunities per Account?","<p>SOQL has no window functions, so use a parent-to-child subquery with ORDER BY and LIMIT, which applies per parent:</p>\n<pre><code>SELECT Id, Name,\n  (SELECT Id, Name, Amount FROM Opportunities\n   WHERE IsClosed = false ORDER BY Amount DESC LIMIT 3)\nFROM Account WHERE Id IN :accIds</code></pre>\n<p>For very large volumes, report with Top N grouping or compute in Batch Apex.</p>"],
["soql-014","Mid","concept","What do ALL ROWS, WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED, FIELDS() and FORMAT() do?","<ul><li><code>ALL ROWS</code>: includes deleted (Recycle Bin) and archived records. <code>WHERE IsDeleted = true</code> to get only deleted.</li>\n<li><code>FIELDS(STANDARD)</code> / <code>FIELDS(ALL)</code> / <code>FIELDS(CUSTOM)</code>: field shortcuts. In Apex only <code>STANDARD</code> works unbounded; ALL/CUSTOM need <code>LIMIT 200</code>.</li>\n<li><code>FORMAT(Amount)</code>, <code>toLabel(StageName)</code>, <code>convertCurrency(Amount)</code>: locale formatting, translated picklist labels, multi-currency conversion.</li>\n<li><code>WITH USER_MODE</code>: enforce user permissions (preferred over SECURITY_ENFORCED).</li></ul>"]]);
SF.add("dml", [["dml-001","Basic","concept","What DML statements are available in Apex?","<p><code>insert</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>upsert</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>undelete</code>, <code>merge</code>, plus the <code>Database</code> class equivalents (<code>Database.insert</code>, <code>Database.upsert</code>, <code>Database.emptyRecycleBin</code>, <code>Database.convertLead</code>). Since Spring '23 you can add access mode: <code>insert as user acc;</code>.</p>"],
["dml-002","Mid","concept","How does upsert decide whether to insert or update?","<ul><li>By default it matches on Salesforce Id; with a field argument it matches on that External Id or unique field: <code>upsert contacts Contact.ERP_Id__c;</code></li>\n<li>No match → insert; one match → update; multiple matches → <code>DUPLICATE_EXTERNAL_ID</code> error for that record.</li>\n<li><code>Database.UpsertResult.isCreated()</code> tells you which happened.</li>\n<li>You can't upsert a generic <code>List&lt;SObject&gt;</code> with an External Id; cast to a concrete list type.</li></ul>"],
["dml-003","Mid","concept","Explain Savepoint and rollback with an example.","<pre><code>Savepoint sp = Database.setSavepoint();\ntry {\n  insert order;\n  for (OrderItem i : items) i.OrderId = order.Id;\n  insert items;\n  Invoice__c inv = InvoiceService.create(order);   // may throw\n} catch (Exception e) {\n  Database.rollback(sp);\n  // Ids assigned to 'order' are NOT cleared: reset before retrying\n  order.Id = null;\n  throw new OrderException('Order creation failed: ' + e.getMessage());\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Each <code>setSavepoint</code> and <code>rollback</code> counts as a DML statement. Static variables are not rolled back. Savepoints can't be used across a callout boundary in some contexts.</p>"],
["dml-004","Mid","concept","How does merge work in Apex?","<pre><code>Account master = [SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Id = :keepId];\nList&lt;Account&gt; dupes = [SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Id IN :dupIds];\nDatabase.MergeResult[] r = Database.merge(master, dupes, false);</code></pre>\n<p>Supported for Account, Contact, Lead, Case and Individual only. Up to 3 records per merge (master + 2). Child records are reparented to the master and losing records are deleted; their delete triggers fire with <code>MasterRecordId</code> set.</p>"],
["dml-005","Senior","concept","In what order should you perform DML on multiple related objects, and how do you reduce DML statements?","<ul><li>Parents first, then children (children need the parent Id), deletes last, so failures roll back cleanly.</li>\n<li>Collect records into lists or Maps and do one DML per object type.</li>\n<li>Insert a parent and child in one statement using an External Id reference:\n<pre><code>Account parent = new Account(Name='Acme', Ext__c='A1');\nContact c = new Contact(LastName='Ray', Account = new Account(Ext__c='A1'));\nDatabase.insert(new List&lt;SObject&gt;{ parent, c });</code></pre></li>\n<li>A mixed <code>List&lt;SObject&gt;</code> with up to 10 object types in one DML is allowed (grouped in chunks; each type switch counts as a chunk).</li>\n<li>Use a Unit of Work pattern for complex services.</li></ul>"],
["dml-006","Mid","concept","What is Database.DMLOptions used for?","<pre><code>Database.DMLOptions o = new Database.DMLOptions();\no.assignmentRuleHeader.useDefaultRule = true;   // run case/lead assignment rules\no.EmailHeader.triggerUserEmail = true;          // send notification email\no.allowFieldTruncation = true;                  // truncate long strings\no.DuplicateRuleHeader.allowSave = true;         // save despite duplicate alert\no.OptAllOrNone = false;\nDatabase.insert(cases, o);</code></pre>"],
["dml-007","Senior","scenario","DML on 15,000 records fails with \"Too many DML rows\". How do you fix it?","<p>The limit is 10,000 records per transaction. Options:</p>\n<ul><li>Move the work to Batch Apex (each execute processes its own 10,000-row budget).</li>\n<li>Split into chained Queueables, each handling a slice.</li>\n<li>If triggered by a user action, process synchronously what's needed now and enqueue the rest.</li>\n<li>Check whether automation is causing extra DML (e.g. a trigger updating child records for every parent).</li></ul>"],
["dml-008","Mid","concept","What happens to Ids and field values after a failed DML in all-or-none mode?","<p>The DML throws <code>DmlException</code>; the records that were inserted before the error in that statement are rolled back, but the Id fields assigned in memory may remain on the sObjects. Before retrying, clear them (<code>rec.Id = null</code>) or create fresh instances. Use <code>e.getNumDml()</code>, <code>e.getDmlIndex(i)</code>, <code>e.getDmlMessage(i)</code> to find which rows failed.</p>"],
["dml-009","Basic","concept","Can you do DML on records returned from a @wire or cacheable Apex method?","<p>A <code>cacheable=true</code> method can't perform DML at all. In LWC the wired data is read-only (frozen), so clone it before editing (<code>{ ...record }</code> or <code>JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(data))</code>), then send changes through an imperative non-cacheable Apex method or <code>updateRecord</code>.</p>"],
["dml-010","Senior","concept","How do you implement a Unit of Work without fflib?","<pre><code>public class SimpleUoW {\n  private Map&lt;SObjectType, List&lt;SObject&gt;&gt; inserts = new Map&lt;SObjectType, List&lt;SObject&gt;&gt;();\n  private Map&lt;SObjectType, Map&lt;Id,SObject&gt;&gt; updates = new Map&lt;SObjectType, Map&lt;Id,SObject&gt;&gt;();\n  private List&lt;SObjectType&gt; order;\n  public SimpleUoW(List&lt;SObjectType&gt; order) { this.order = order; }\n  public void registerNew(SObject r) {\n    SObjectType t = r.getSObjectType();\n    if (!inserts.containsKey(t)) inserts.put(t, new List&lt;SObject&gt;());\n    inserts.get(t).add(r);\n  }\n  public void registerDirty(SObject r) {\n    SObjectType t = r.getSObjectType();\n    if (!updates.containsKey(t)) updates.put(t, new Map&lt;Id,SObject&gt;());\n    updates.get(t).put(r.Id, r);   // de-duplicates by Id\n  }\n  public void commitWork() {\n    Savepoint sp = Database.setSavepoint();\n    try {\n      for (SObjectType t : order) if (inserts.containsKey(t)) insert inserts.get(t);\n      for (SObjectType t : order) if (updates.containsKey(t)) update updates.get(t).values();\n    } catch (Exception e) { Database.rollback(sp); throw e; }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Keys: one DML per type, parent-first ordering, Id de-duplication (avoids \"Duplicate id in list\"), single rollback point.</p>"],
["dml-011","Mid","concept","What causes \"Duplicate id in list\" and how do you avoid it?","<p>Updating a List that contains the same record Id twice. It's common when several loops add the same parent to an update list. Use a <code>Map&lt;Id, SObject&gt;</code> and update <code>map.values()</code>, merging field changes into one instance per Id.</p>"]]);
SF.add("criteria", [["criteria-001","Mid","code","When an Opportunity is Closed Won, create a follow-up Task for the owner due in 7 days. Avoid duplicates.","<pre><code>// after update handler\nList&lt;Task&gt; tasks = new List&lt;Task&gt;();\nfor (Opportunity o : newList) {\n  Opportunity old = oldMap.get(o.Id);\n  if (o.StageName == 'Closed Won' &amp;&amp; old.StageName != 'Closed Won') {\n    tasks.add(new Task(\n      WhatId = o.Id, OwnerId = o.OwnerId,\n      Subject = 'Onboarding call: ' + o.Name,\n      ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),\n      Priority = 'High', Status = 'Not Started'));\n  }\n}\nif (!tasks.isEmpty()) insert tasks;</code></pre>\n<p>Criteria checks the transition (old != new), so re-saving a won deal doesn't create another task. Include after insert too if deals can be created already won.</p>"],
["criteria-002","Mid","code","Prevent deletion of an Account that has open Cases, unless the user has a custom permission.","<pre><code>// before delete\nif (FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Delete_Accounts_With_Cases')) return;\nSet&lt;Id&gt; blocked = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\nfor (AggregateResult ar : [SELECT AccountId a FROM Case\n     WHERE AccountId IN :oldMap.keySet() AND IsClosed = false GROUP BY AccountId]) {\n  blocked.add((Id) ar.get('a'));\n}\nfor (Account a : oldList) {\n  if (blocked.contains(a.Id)) a.addError('This account has open cases. Close them before deleting.');\n}</code></pre>"],
["criteria-003","Mid","code","When an Account's Billing Address changes, copy it to all related Contacts' Mailing Address — but only Contacts where Use_Account_Address__c is true.","<pre><code>// after update on Account\nMap&lt;Id, Account&gt; changed = new Map&lt;Id, Account&gt;();\nfor (Account a : newList) {\n  Account o = oldMap.get(a.Id);\n  if (a.BillingStreet != o.BillingStreet || a.BillingCity != o.BillingCity ||\n      a.BillingPostalCode != o.BillingPostalCode || a.BillingCountry != o.BillingCountry ||\n      a.BillingState != o.BillingState) changed.put(a.Id, a);\n}\nif (changed.isEmpty()) return;\nList&lt;Contact&gt; cons = [SELECT Id, AccountId FROM Contact\n  WHERE AccountId IN :changed.keySet() AND Use_Account_Address__c = true];\nfor (Contact c : cons) {\n  Account a = changed.get(c.AccountId);\n  c.MailingStreet = a.BillingStreet; c.MailingCity = a.BillingCity;\n  c.MailingState = a.BillingState; c.MailingPostalCode = a.BillingPostalCode;\n  c.MailingCountry = a.BillingCountry;\n}\nupdate cons;</code></pre>\n<p>Follow-up the interviewer may ask: if an Account has 50,000 contacts, move this to a Queueable/Batch.</p>"],
["criteria-004","Mid","code","Only one Contact per Account can be marked Primary__c = true. Enforce it.","<pre><code>// before insert, before update on Contact\nMap&lt;Id, Contact&gt; newPrimary = new Map&lt;Id, Contact&gt;();\nfor (Contact c : newList) {\n  if (c.Primary__c &amp;&amp; c.AccountId != null) {\n    if (newPrimary.containsKey(c.AccountId)) {\n      c.addError('Only one primary contact per account in this save.');\n    } else newPrimary.put(c.AccountId, c);\n  }\n}\nif (newPrimary.isEmpty()) return;\nSet&lt;Id&gt; selfIds = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\nfor (Contact c : newPrimary.values()) if (c.Id != null) selfIds.add(c.Id);\nList&lt;Contact&gt; existing = [SELECT Id FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :newPrimary.keySet()\n                          AND Primary__c = true AND Id NOT IN :selfIds];\n// Option A: block\n// Option B (usually preferred): uncheck the old primary in an after trigger\nfor (Contact e : existing) e.Primary__c = false;\nContactTriggerHandler.pendingUpdates.addAll(existing);   // update in after context</code></pre>\n<p>Discuss the choice: block vs auto-uncheck. Auto-uncheck needs the update in the after phase plus a recursion guard.</p>"],
["criteria-005","Mid","code","If a Case is High priority and the Account is a Platinum customer, assign it to the Platinum queue and set the SLA due date to 4 business hours from now.","<pre><code>// before insert on Case\nSet&lt;Id&gt; accIds = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\nfor (Case c : newList) if (c.AccountId != null &amp;&amp; c.Priority == 'High') accIds.add(c.AccountId);\nif (accIds.isEmpty()) return;\nMap&lt;Id, Account&gt; accs = new Map&lt;Id, Account&gt;(\n  [SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accIds AND Tier__c = 'Platinum']);\nId queueId = [SELECT Id FROM Group WHERE Type = 'Queue' AND DeveloperName = 'Platinum_Support' LIMIT 1].Id;\nBusinessHours bh = [SELECT Id FROM BusinessHours WHERE IsDefault = true];\nfor (Case c : newList) {\n  if (c.Priority == 'High' &amp;&amp; accs.containsKey(c.AccountId)) {\n    c.OwnerId = queueId;\n    c.SLA_Due__c = BusinessHours.add(bh.Id, System.now(), 4 * 60 * 60 * 1000L);\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Better in production: store the queue name in Custom Metadata, and consider Entitlements + Omni-Channel instead of hard-coding.</p>"],
["criteria-006","Senior","code","Roll up the total Amount of Closed Won Opportunities to a custom field on Account using a lookup (not master-detail). Handle every case.","<pre><code>trigger OppRollup on Opportunity (after insert, after update, after delete, after undelete) {\n  Set&lt;Id&gt; accIds = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\n  if (Trigger.isInsert || Trigger.isUndelete) {\n    for (Opportunity o : Trigger.new) if (o.AccountId != null) accIds.add(o.AccountId);\n  } else if (Trigger.isDelete) {\n    for (Opportunity o : Trigger.old) if (o.AccountId != null) accIds.add(o.AccountId);\n  } else {\n    for (Opportunity o : Trigger.new) {\n      Opportunity old = Trigger.oldMap.get(o.Id);\n      if (o.Amount != old.Amount || o.StageName != old.StageName || o.AccountId != old.AccountId) {\n        if (o.AccountId != null) accIds.add(o.AccountId);\n        if (old.AccountId != null) accIds.add(old.AccountId);   // reparented\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  if (accIds.isEmpty()) return;\n  Map&lt;Id, Account&gt; upd = new Map&lt;Id, Account&gt;();\n  for (Id i : accIds) upd.put(i, new Account(Id = i, Won_Amount__c = 0));\n  for (AggregateResult ar : [SELECT AccountId a, SUM(Amount) s FROM Opportunity\n       WHERE AccountId IN :accIds AND StageName = 'Closed Won' GROUP BY AccountId]) {\n    upd.get((Id) ar.get('a')).Won_Amount__c = (Decimal) ar.get('s');\n  }\n  update upd.values();\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Covers insert, amount/stage change, reparenting, delete, undelete, and resets to 0 when no children qualify. Mention lock contention on hot accounts and the async option.</p>"],
["criteria-007","Mid","code","When a Lead is created with an email that matches an existing Contact, link it and mark it as a duplicate instead of blocking.","<pre><code>// before insert on Lead\nSet&lt;String&gt; emails = new Set&lt;String&gt;();\nfor (Lead l : newList) if (String.isNotBlank(l.Email)) emails.add(l.Email.toLowerCase());\nif (emails.isEmpty()) return;\nMap&lt;String, Contact&gt; byEmail = new Map&lt;String, Contact&gt;();\nfor (Contact c : [SELECT Id, Email, AccountId FROM Contact WHERE Email IN :emails]) {\n  byEmail.put(c.Email.toLowerCase(), c);\n}\nfor (Lead l : newList) {\n  Contact m = l.Email == null ? null : byEmail.get(l.Email.toLowerCase());\n  if (m != null) { l.Existing_Contact__c = m.Id; l.Status = 'Duplicate'; }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Note: SOQL equality on Email is case-insensitive, but the Map lookup isn't, so normalise keys.</p>"],
["criteria-008","Senior","code","Every night, deactivate users who haven't logged in for 90 days, except System Administrators and integration users, and email their managers.","<pre><code>public class InactiveUserJob implements Database.Batchable&lt;SObject&gt;, Schedulable, Database.Stateful {\n  List&lt;String&gt; deactivated = new List&lt;String&gt;();\n  public Database.QueryLocator start(Database.BatchableContext bc) {\n    Datetime cutoff = System.now().addDays(-90);\n    return Database.getQueryLocator([SELECT Id, Name, ManagerId, Manager.Email FROM User\n      WHERE IsActive = true AND UserType = 'Standard'\n      AND (LastLoginDate &lt; :cutoff OR LastLoginDate = null)\n      AND CreatedDate &lt; :cutoff\n      AND Profile.Name != 'System Administrator'\n      AND Is_Integration_User__c = false]);\n  }\n  public void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List&lt;User&gt; scope) {\n    List&lt;Messaging.SingleEmailMessage&gt; mails = new List&lt;Messaging.SingleEmailMessage&gt;();\n    for (User u : scope) {\n      u.IsActive = false; deactivated.add(u.Name);\n      if (u.ManagerId != null) {\n        Messaging.SingleEmailMessage m = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();\n        m.setTargetObjectId(u.ManagerId); m.setSaveAsActivity(false);\n        m.setSubject('User deactivated: ' + u.Name);\n        m.setPlainTextBody(u.Name + ' was deactivated after 90 days without login.');\n        mails.add(m);\n      }\n    }\n    Database.update(scope, false);\n    if (!mails.isEmpty()) Messaging.sendEmail(mails, false);\n  }\n  public void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) { /* summary to admins */ }\n  public void execute(SchedulableContext sc) { Database.executeBatch(this, 100); }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Talking points: user DML is setup DML (keep it separate from other objects), users who own queues/workflows may fail to deactivate (use partial success and log), freezing (<code>UserLogin.IsFrozen</code>) as a softer alternative.</p>"],
["criteria-009","Mid","code","Write a SOQL query: Accounts in India with more than 3 Contacts and at least one open Case created this month.","<pre><code>SELECT Id, Name FROM Account\nWHERE BillingCountry = 'India'\nAND Id IN (SELECT AccountId FROM Case WHERE IsClosed = false AND CreatedDate = THIS_MONTH)\nAND Id IN (SELECT AccountId FROM Contact GROUP BY AccountId HAVING COUNT(Id) &gt; 3)</code></pre>\n<p>The second semi-join with GROUP BY/HAVING isn't allowed in SOQL. Correct approach: run an aggregate query for the contact counts first, then filter:</p>\n<pre><code>Set&lt;Id&gt; many = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\nfor (AggregateResult ar : [SELECT AccountId a FROM Contact WHERE Account.BillingCountry = 'India'\n     GROUP BY AccountId HAVING COUNT(Id) &gt; 3]) many.add((Id) ar.get('a'));\nList&lt;Account&gt; r = [SELECT Id, Name FROM Account WHERE Id IN :many\n  AND Id IN (SELECT AccountId FROM Case WHERE IsClosed = false AND CreatedDate = THIS_MONTH)];</code></pre>\n<p class=\"tip\">Interviewers like this one because the first version looks right but doesn't compile.</p>"],
["criteria-010","Mid","code","Write a query to find duplicate Contacts by email in the org.","<pre><code>SELECT Email, COUNT(Id) cnt FROM Contact\nWHERE Email != null\nGROUP BY Email\nHAVING COUNT(Id) &gt; 1\nORDER BY COUNT(Id) DESC</code></pre>\n<p>Over 50,000 contacts, aggregate queries can hit limits; run it in Batch Apex, in the Developer Console, or use Duplicate Jobs (Performance/Unlimited editions).</p>"],
["criteria-011","Senior","code","When a Quote is approved, sync its line items to the Opportunity, replacing existing products, and stop if the Opportunity is Closed.","<pre><code>public with sharing class QuoteSyncService {\n  public static void sync(Set&lt;Id&gt; quoteIds) {\n    Map&lt;Id, Quote&gt; quotes = new Map&lt;Id, Quote&gt;([SELECT Id, OpportunityId, Opportunity.IsClosed,\n      (SELECT PricebookEntryId, Quantity, UnitPrice, Discount FROM QuoteLineItems)\n      FROM Quote WHERE Id IN :quoteIds]);\n    Set&lt;Id&gt; oppIds = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\n    List&lt;OpportunityLineItem&gt; newLines = new List&lt;OpportunityLineItem&gt;();\n    for (Quote q : quotes.values()) {\n      if (q.Opportunity.IsClosed) continue;\n      oppIds.add(q.OpportunityId);\n      for (QuoteLineItem l : q.QuoteLineItems) newLines.add(new OpportunityLineItem(\n        OpportunityId = q.OpportunityId, PricebookEntryId = l.PricebookEntryId,\n        Quantity = l.Quantity, UnitPrice = l.UnitPrice, Discount = l.Discount));\n    }\n    Savepoint sp = Database.setSavepoint();\n    try {\n      delete [SELECT Id FROM OpportunityLineItem WHERE OpportunityId IN :oppIds];\n      insert newLines;\n    } catch (DmlException e) { Database.rollback(sp); throw e; }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Mention the native \"Start Sync\" feature (<code>Opportunity.SyncedQuoteId</code>) as the declarative alternative.</p>"],
["criteria-012","Mid","code","Create an LWC that shows open Cases for the current Account and lets the agent close one with a reason.","<pre><code>// accountCases.js\nimport { LightningElement, api, wire } from 'lwc';\nimport { refreshApex } from '@salesforce/apex';\nimport getOpenCases from '@salesforce/apex/CaseCtrl.getOpenCases';\nimport closeCase from '@salesforce/apex/CaseCtrl.closeCase';\nexport default class AccountCases extends LightningElement {\n  @api recordId; wired; cases; reason = '';\n  columns = [{ label: 'Number', fieldName: 'CaseNumber' }, { label: 'Subject', fieldName: 'Subject' },\n             { type: 'button', typeAttributes: { label: 'Close', name: 'close' } }];\n  @wire(getOpenCases, { accountId: '$recordId' })\n  load(r) { this.wired = r; this.cases = r.data; }\n  handleReason(e) { this.reason = e.target.value; }\n  async handleRow(e) {\n    if (!this.reason) return;\n    await closeCase({ caseId: e.detail.row.Id, reason: this.reason });\n    await refreshApex(this.wired);\n  }\n}\n// CaseCtrl.cls\n@AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)\npublic static List&lt;Case&gt; getOpenCases(Id accountId) {\n  return [SELECT Id, CaseNumber, Subject FROM Case WHERE AccountId = :accountId\n          AND IsClosed = false WITH USER_MODE ORDER BY CreatedDate DESC LIMIT 50];\n}\n@AuraEnabled\npublic static void closeCase(Id caseId, String reason) {\n  update as user new Case(Id = caseId, Status = 'Closed', Close_Reason__c = reason);\n}</code></pre>"],
["criteria-013","Senior","code","If more than 5 Cases are created for the same Account within 1 hour, create a single escalation record and notify the account owner. Must work with bulk loads.","<pre><code>// after insert on Case\nSet&lt;Id&gt; accIds = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\nfor (Case c : newList) if (c.AccountId != null) accIds.add(c.AccountId);\nif (accIds.isEmpty()) return;\nDatetime since = System.now().addHours(-1);\nSet&lt;Id&gt; alreadyEscalated = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();\nfor (Escalation__c e : [SELECT Account__c FROM Escalation__c\n     WHERE Account__c IN :accIds AND CreatedDate &gt;= :since]) alreadyEscalated.add(e.Account__c);\nList&lt;Escalation__c&gt; esc = new List&lt;Escalation__c&gt;();\nfor (AggregateResult ar : [SELECT AccountId a, COUNT(Id) n FROM Case\n     WHERE AccountId IN :accIds AND CreatedDate &gt;= :since GROUP BY AccountId HAVING COUNT(Id) &gt; 5]) {\n  Id a = (Id) ar.get('a');\n  if (!alreadyEscalated.contains(a)) esc.add(new Escalation__c(Account__c = a, Case_Count__c = (Integer) ar.get('n')));\n}\ninsert esc;   // notification via Flow on Escalation__c or Custom Notification</code></pre>\n<p>Edge cases to raise: concurrent transactions both creating an escalation (use a unique key field like AccountId + hour bucket), and rolling window vs fixed hour.</p>"],
["criteria-014","Mid","code","Write a validation rule: Discount over 20% requires a Justification, and over 40% only Sales Managers can save it.","<pre><code>/* Rule 1 */\nAND( Discount__c &gt; 0.20, ISBLANK(Discount_Justification__c) )\n\n/* Rule 2 */\nAND( Discount__c &gt; 0.40,\n     $UserRole.DeveloperName != 'Sales_Manager',\n     NOT($Permission.Approve_High_Discount) )</code></pre>\n<p>Use a custom permission instead of a role name when possible, so the rule survives role restructuring. Use <code>ISCHANGED(Discount__c)</code> if old records with high discounts should still be editable.</p>"]]);
